window.harnessChunks["rows-c492404dc0009b26.js"]=["<tr data-a=\"1\" data-c=\"1\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-10-31T18:36:24+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/Doorman11991/smallcode\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">SmallCode</a><small>2026</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"1\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>1</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>1</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-10-31\">2026-10-31</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/Doorman11991/smallcode/tree/624970cce81657fd2751ee7f11ba85f35f2bb5c3\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision 624970cce81657fd2751ee7f11ba85f35f2bb5c3\">624970c &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: produce a software change or informed answer in the working repository. - Disturbance / variety regulated: unknown code, tool failures, invalid edits and incomplete task information. - Decisive decision or feedback right: choose the next inspection/action and revise the implementation from returned observations. - Decision owner: the model-driven SmallCode coding actor. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: tool-call parsing, executor, context/plan state, validation and retry limits. - Closure path: model response → tool execution → tool observations/validation errors → next model decision and file changes. - Boundary reachability: the packaged CLI invokes runAgentLoop; the model/tool loop and validation feedback are active in normal task execution. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: the model chooses concrete work; the runtime transports and constrains those choices. - Evidence: CLI loop and feedback, executor, architecture. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: source assessment; no live model performance claim.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: — - Function: no closed inter-S1 coordination relation is supplied in the assessed installed session. - Disturbance / variety regulated: same-file parallel edits are recognized by the dependency graph, but no active parallel-worker path closes their regulation here. - Decisive decision or feedback right: no in-boundary autonomous coordination owner established. - Decision owner: not established; dependency extraction and batching are deterministic. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: plan graph and dormant parallel-executor exports. - Closure path: batch structure is stored/displayed, not dispatched to parallel units by the reviewed CLI. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: plan sequencing and exported mini-loops do not establish a reachable coordinating actor. - Evidence: dependency graph, parallel executor, CLI graph call sites. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: medium-high. - Caveats: the exported executor could support future composition; that is not the assessed shipped orchestration.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: — - Function: no separate whole-system current-control loop established. - Disturbance / variety regulated: local model failure, validation errors and context exhaustion remain coding-session operational variety. - Decisive decision or feedback right: fixed limits and fallback thresholds do not demonstrate resource/commitment bargaining on behalf of a multi-unit whole. - Decision owner: developer/operator selects configuration; coding actor chooses local repair. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: governor, token monitor, early-stop, hard-fail checks and escalation. - Closure path: failures return to the coding loop; no distinct whole-system current-control decision is reconstructed. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: autonomous task repair is S1; budget enforcement alone is not S3. - Evidence: governor, budget policy, escalation. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: medium-high. - Caveats: no S3 parent mode is inferred from changing a setting.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-c\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: C - Function: complementary challenge of a coding actor&#39;s implementation/completion claims using independently inspected code and checks. - Disturbance / variety regulated: producer summaries can omit defects or unverified requirements. - Decisive decision or feedback right: the critic decides OKAY/REJECT from its own source inspection; the caller must compose the corrective return to the producer. - Decision owner: the separately invoked critic actor owns audit judgment; the operator/developer owns invocation and subsequent corrective integration. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: isolated AgentRunner context, narrowed read/search/bash/test tools, bounded steps and command output. - Closure path: /agent critic or /team review → isolated inspection → finding/verdict returned to the caller; automatic re-entry into the production coding actor is not wired, hence C rather than A. - Boundary reachability: bin/commands.js invokes AgentRunner/runTeam using shipped agent/team definitions. This is an optional CLI mode, not repository-development dogfood. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: the audit-specific constructor supplies a real independent tool-using judgment path, while corrective ownership/closure still needs composition. - Claim being audited: implementation satisfies the requirements and is ready to ship. - Ordinary reporting path: coding actor response and routine post-edit validation. - Complementary access path: critic reads the spec and changed files and runs checks; red-team can separately inspect attack surfaces. - Independence boundary: AgentRunner starts a new bounded conversation without parent history and permits raw artifact access. Different model tiers are optional; independence rests on evidence access and separate judgment. - Who acts on findings: caller/operator must return findings for repair; team pipeline alone prints the final result. - Evidence: critic, isolated runner, command wiring, team runner. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: medium-high. - Caveats: the asynchronous summary-only reviewer is insufficient by itself and is not the credited path. Merely running the sequential review team does not prove autonomous corrective audit closure.\">C</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-p\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: P - Function: adapt reusable coding capability to recurring unmet user demand beyond a single task. - Disturbance / variety regulated: repeated requests that lack a corresponding skill and recurrent tool friction in prior sessions. - Decisive decision or feedback right: select whether a proposed reusable skill should become active in future sessions. - Decision owner: the legitimate local operator; the model generates an option, but /evolve promote is an explicit operator decision. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: trace storage, friction extraction, model proposal, proposal validation, draft storage, promotion and skill loading. - Closure path: /evolve examines saved demand/friction → model proposes one reusable skill → operator reviews and /evolve promote activates it for subsequent sessions → the skill can govern later task execution. - Boundary reachability: /evolve and /evolve promote are registered shipped CLI commands; promoted drafts enter the normal SkillManager skill directory. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: option generation is model-assisted, but the decisive adaptation right remains parent-owned. No autonomous A/C base is claimed from a human-required promotion path. - External distinction: repeated user requests not covered by existing skill keywords distinguish unmet demand in the harness&#39;s local user/task environment. - Future / prospective distinction: the proposed skill is reusable guidance for later similar work, rather than a patch only to the current task. - Adaptation option generated: one named, validated skill proposal with trigger, keywords, body and rationale. - Path back into current capability / S3: operator promotion moves the draft into active skills; ordinary skill selection/injection then affects later operation. No separate autonomous S3 is inferred. - Evidence: command proposal/promotion, demand/friction extraction, skills activation, proposal machinery. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: medium. - Caveats: only the demand-driven reusable-skill path is credited; generic evidence memory, task planning and reactive model routing do not independently establish S4. No claim of measured adaptation benefit.\">P</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: — - Function: no runtime identity/ultimate-policy adjudication loop established. - Disturbance / variety regulated: fixed task constraints, permissions and configured provider limits. - Decisive decision or feedback right: configuration and user task commands do not constitute an identity-level proposal/decision/return path. - Decision owner: operator/developer supplies constraints; no qualifying S5 owner is reconstructed. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: prompts, configuration, budget and permission guards. - Closure path: operational commands/configuration affect execution; no ultimate-policy escalation/returned decision is established. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: static boundaries and ordinary human intervention do not imply S5. - Evidence: configuration, commands, CLI prompts/guards. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: medium-high. - Caveats: repository maintainers&#39; authority is outside the installed-session recursion.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/2ca00f8a9c9f29912e92f100a9292d18b2f55de5/assessments/sm/smallcode.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit 2ca00f8a9c9f29912e92f100a9292d18b2f55de5\">2ca00f8 &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr data-a=\"1\" data-c=\"0\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-09-18T21:27:31+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/alibaba/open-code-review\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">OpenCodeReview</a><small>2026</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"1\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>1</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>0</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-09-18\">2026-09-18</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/alibaba/open-code-review/tree/7a571b78d3493b249f6ad14d835c6a79a0a67d2e\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision 7a571b78d3493b249f6ad14d835c6a79a0a67d2e\">7a571b7 &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A. - Function: inspect assigned code/diff material and produce substantive review findings. - Disturbance / variety regulated: language/framework variation, repository context, defect/risk ambiguity, cross-file evidence and changing source content. - Decisive decision or feedback right: the review agent decides which potential issues warrant comments and which context/tools to use before finishing its subtask. - Decision owner: model-driven OCR-managed review agent. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: deterministic file selection, bundling, rules, tool registry, line anchoring and comment worker pool. - Closure path: agent inspection/tool results change subsequent reasoning; accepted codecomment results become the delivered review output. - Basis /\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: —. - Multiple file-group subagents can run with isolated contexts and concurrency, but the pinned evidence does not establish a specific mutual interference/conflict/oscillation among operational S1 units plus a feedback relation that regulates it. Divide-and-conquer bundling and asynchronous scheduling alone are not S2. -\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: —. - Deterministic orchestration controls selection, grouping, rule application and scheduling, but no distinct autonomous actor has both a current whole-review view and discretionary authority over shared organizational resources, commitments or priorities. Pipeline control is not promoted to S3. -\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: —. - The reflection/positioning machinery is separate in implementation but runs as part of the ordinary codecomment production/post-processing path. The reviewed evidence does not reconstruct a sufficiently independent complementary access path that challenges normal operational reporting and returns findings to a distinct current-control owner. - Put differently, “independent module” is not sufficient by itself: reflection here is routine quality processing of the review product, and no separate corrective organizational loop is established at this boundary. -\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: —. - Repository search, dynamic context retrieval and production-derived tool tuning improve review execution but do not form an external-and-prospective environmental intelligence loop with adaptation options returned into current organizational capability. -\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: —. - Review rules, configured models/providers and operator-selected execution modes constrain the product, but no runtime identity/ultimate-policy authority and return loop is established. Human consumption/disposition of review comments is outside the supplied harness governance path. -\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/4155c4340e1b7a5ca09b5decae990b0a6948420f/assessments/open-code-review.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit 4155c4340e1b7a5ca09b5decae990b0a6948420f\">4155c43 &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr data-a=\"1\" data-c=\"0\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-10-06T13:42:27+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/p0systems/peezy-cli\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Peezy</a><small>2026</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"1\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>1</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>0</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-10-06\">2026-10-06</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/p0systems/peezy-cli/tree/4abb65bb3e8c9f6c4cdd42c22d29b61531bffad3\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision 4abb65bb3e8c9f6c4cdd42c22d29b61531bffad3\">4abb65b &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: autonomously inspect, modify and verify a software project using model-selected coding/tool actions. - Disturbance / variety regulated: unfamiliar repository state, implementation choices, tool/process failures, context pressure, provider behavior and denied actions. - Decisive decision or feedback right: choose substantive coding/tool actions, interpret their outcomes, adapt after failures/denials and decide when the task is complete. - Decision owner: the active model-backed Peezy agent. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: ToolRegistry, gateway streaming, approvals, path confinement, todo state, compaction, session persistence/resume, MCP registration and interrupt handling. - Closure path: user objective → model selects tool/coding action → runtime executes or returns denial/error → result re-enters model history → model revises work → final answer closes the turn. - Boundary reachability: both interactive and headless entry points construct the same first-party Agent and tool registry; headless changes approval handling without replacing the core decision loop. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: runtime code enforces permissions and transports tool results, but the model owns the open-ended coding decisions and evidence interpretation. - Evidence: README.md; src/core/agent.ts; src/core/create-agent.ts. - Basis: explicit + structural. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: ask/plan modes can constrain execution, but that does not transfer the coding decision itself away from the model.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: — - Function: no material inter-S1 coordination function is established. - Disturbance / variety regulated: none at the declared recursion because the standard runtime does not instantiate distinct concurrent operational agents. - Decisive decision or feedback right: none established. - Decision owner: none established. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: todo sequencing, MCP tool registration and tool execution order remain inside one S1 loop. - Closure path: not applicable. - Boundary reachability: no positive S2 path claimed. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: generic task ordering/tool extensibility is not peer interference attenuation. - Evidence: src/core/agent.ts; src/core/tools/todo.ts; src/core/mcp/manager.ts. - Basis: structural absence review. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: connected MCP servers may themselves contain agents, but external systems cannot donate S2 to Peezy.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: — - Function: no material whole-system current-control function is established. - Disturbance / variety regulated: no portfolio of distinct current operational commitments exists under a separate controller. - Decisive decision or feedback right: none established. - Decision owner: none established. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: approval prompts, request caps, abort signals, plan-mode mutation rejection and todo visibility constrain one S1 turn. - Closure path: not applicable. - Boundary reachability: no positive S3 path claimed. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: human approval is action-local permission and interrupt is termination enforcement; neither provides a whole-system current view plus substantive control over resources/commitments. - Evidence: src/core/agent.ts; src/core/tools/safety.ts; README.md. - Basis: structural absence review. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: generic approval or stop controls are explicitly insufficient for S3 under Methodology 0.3.6.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: — - Function: no material independent complementary audit path is established. - Disturbance / variety regulated: no separate actor independently challenges an operational claim using a complementary evidence path. - Decisive decision or feedback right: none established. - Decision owner: none established. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: the same coding agent may run tests, inspect diffs or use read tools; plan mode and todo tracking do not create independent review. - Closure path: not applicable. - Boundary reachability: no positive S3 path claimed. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: no fresh reviewer/verifier role or separate evidence channel is wired into the standard distribution. - Evidence: src/core/prompt/system.ts; src/core/agent.ts. - Basis: structural absence review. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: a user can ask the same agent or an external MCP tool to review; generic composition is not a first-party independent audit loop.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: — - Function: no material external-and-prospective adaptation loop is established. - Disturbance / variety regulated: no future/external distinction is transformed into an adaptation option that changes current organizational capability. - Decisive decision or feedback right: none established. - Decision owner: none established. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: session resume, compaction, provider/model configuration, live MCP changes and project instructions preserve/reconfigure context but do not themselves close S4. - Closure path: not applicable. - Boundary reachability: no positive S4 path claimed. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: compaction summarizes prior context and configuration selects tools/models; neither is an autonomous prospective adaptation owner. - Evidence: src/core/prompt/compaction.ts; src/core/session/store.ts; src/core/create-agent.ts. - Basis: structural absence review. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: persistent experience can inform later S1 turns, but persistence/context reuse is insufficient S4.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: — - Function: no material identity/ultimate-policy governance loop is established. - Disturbance / variety regulated: no identity-level policy matter is routed to an authoritative owner and returned as governing runtime policy. - Decisive decision or feedback right: none established. - Decision owner: none established. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: compiled system prompt, approval modes, path confinement, project instructions and provider/tool configuration constrain operations. - Closure path: not applicable. - Boundary reachability: no positive S5 path claimed. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Peezy operates within user/developer-authored rules and has no authority to redefine its identity or ultimate policy. - Evidence: src/core/prompt/system.ts; src/core/agent.ts; README.md. - Basis: structural absence review. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: action approvals are not identity governance.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/1990b5aa53ad357c8c87753b6ceaccd697711001/assessments/peezy.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit 1990b5aa53ad357c8c87753b6ceaccd697711001\">1990b5a &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr data-a=\"1\" data-c=\"0\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-10-13T21:55:32+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/zap-coding-agent/zap-coding-agent\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Zap</a><small>2026</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"1\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>1</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>0</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-10-13\">2026-10-13</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/zap-coding-agent/zap-coding-agent/tree/9812a142b1d12c894c98a6f5d08cc776ecc760da\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision 9812a142b1d12c894c98a6f5d08cc776ecc760da\">9812a14 &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: choose and carry out codebase engineering work under uncertainty, including source inspection, modifications, shell/test execution and error-driven repairs. - Disturbance / variety regulated: unknown program structure and test behavior, failed commands, tool limitations, insufficient context and unexpected code interactions. - Decisive decision or feedback right: model selects the next specific tool/argument or correction after observing actual results; invoked child runs its own independent model-tool loop. - Decision owner: active coding model in Session (or in a delegated child), not shell/tool dispatcher, task-index or watchdog. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: LLM client, session context and turn loop, code index, file/shell tools, permission gate, turn and verification budgets. - Closure path: user goal → model-selected read/edit/execute action → source or shell result → next model turn or completion; tool failure may trigger model-directed revision. - Boundary reachability: normal CLI/TUI session and first-party spawnagent registered in the default non-child session runtime. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: the same deterministic tools alone cannot invent appropriate engineering actions without a model decision. - Evidence: README.md; ARCHITECTURE.md; turn.rs; tools.rs; mod.rs; agent.rs; agentcore.rs - Basis: explicit / structural -\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: — - Function: no specific observed or source-wired first-party attenuation of material inter-S1 interference with returned behavior change is established. - Disturbance / variety regulated: independent write-capable delegated children can collide in a shared working tree; duplicative work and resource contention are plausible inter-worker disturbances. - Decisive decision or feedback right: no inspected controller detects such a concrete collision and limits or reallocates participating child work; source tool&#39;s filesinscope is not consumed by the dispatch path. - Decision owner: parent model verbally instructed to split independent work, with deterministic tool batching; neither is an executed S2 conflict resolution owner. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: spawnagent prompts, optional filesinscope declaration, per-child session isolation, tool call parallelism, user-operated background session tracker. - Closure path: child task may return a result to the parent; no independently evidenced conflict report → attenuation affecting the conflicting children → updated child work closure. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: a concurrent spawnagent response and “do not edit same file” instruction do not detect and settle actual inter-S1 interference, and UI background status is not a corrective channel. - Evidence: agent.rs; agentcore.rs; tools.rs; backgroundagent.rs; mod.rs; ARCHITECTURE.md - Basis: structural and explicit execute argument usage -\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: — - Function: no separately evidenced current whole-organization managerial decision for the live roster/commitments of subordinate coding S1s. - Disturbance / variety regulated: failing delegated work, conflicting team priorities and resource/commitment tradeoffs of several simultaneous coding workers. - Decisive decision or feedback right: parent can issue additional isolated child subtasks and consume their final answers; no built-in whole-current roster view and discretionary active priority reassignment applied to existing model-running child tasks was found. - Decision owner: parent coding model controls its present task decomposition, while /bg list/start/kill remains a user operation. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: per-session task lists, user background agent status panel, automatic model routing by task classifier, plan step runner and tool watchdog. - Closure path: delegated output returns to parent coding turn, and user background status to UI; no documented active whole-team status → discretionary control choice → changed current commitments across children and closed return. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: decomposition of one coding request into independent calls, or one process&#39;s retries/verification, is S1 strategy and task execution rather than whole-current organizational control. - Evidence: agent.rs; backgroundagent.rs; mod.rs; taskplanner.rs; planexecution.rs; watchdog.rs - Basis: structural -\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: — - Function: no separately owned independent source-first auditor forms a corrective challenge to a coding worker&#39;s claims and returns to a distinct current-control manager within the shipped organization. - Disturbance / variety regulated: a coding agent&#39;s potentially false edit/test-success claims, missed regressions or unsafe modifications. - Decisive decision or feedback right: watchdog detects repeated failing verification commands, and plans require tests, but the same coding loop acts on those outputs; no separately commissioned independent auditor verdict and managerial corrective return is wired as a first-party mode. - Decision owner: normal code verification remains with originating coding model and deterministic watchdog; human/CI reviewers are outside runtime. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: watchdog.rs per-command fail ledger/nudges and tool withdrawal, planexecution.rs step verification rules, shell output, user-visible audit log. - Closure path: test failure returns to the same coding actor and can alter its next step or cause stop; no independent reviewer → distinct manager → affected coder corrective cycle. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: watchdog and editing-plan QA are task execution reliability protections, not separate complementary audit ownership. - Evidence: watchdog.rs; planexecution.rs; tools.rs; turn.rs; agent.rs; ARCHITECTURE.md - Basis: structural -\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: — - Function: no agentic outside/future-oriented organizational capability strategy plus applied capacity adaptation cycle. - Disturbance / variety regulated: changing coding requirements, future language support, provider/skill constraints and environment capability. - Decisive decision or feedback right: no separate actor selects long-horizon capability changes from external signals and implements them into the agent organization. - Decision owner: developer/operator configures provider, skill/index settings and deployments; model addresses the current coding task. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: AST code-index, dynamic skill matching and context refresh, task planning, persistence and provider routing. - Closure path: contexts or skills inform current action, but no future organizational-capability choice → decision → implementation → feedback loop is evidenced. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: adaptive context retrieval and task-level planning are not equivalent to future-capacity intelligence. - Evidence: mod.rs; skillmanager.rs; memoryrefresh.rs; taskplanner.rs; mod.rs; contextmanager.rs - Basis: structural -\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: — - Function: no first-party ultimate-purpose/policy authority over the agent organization is demonstrated. - Disturbance / variety regulated: mission/identity tradeoffs distinct from per-command safety decisions. - Decisive decision or feedback right: no S5 owner arbitrates organizational purpose and returns binding governing change to multiple S1s. - Decision owner: user/operator and developer configuration set purpose/permissions; built-in permission gate and watchdog enforce narrower runtime safety conditions. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: permissions, destructive-command checks, sandbox, task goals, user config and runtime tool policy. - Closure path: allow/deny a shell or file operation, nudge/stop a repeatedly failing agent and return technical status; no higher-purpose identity decision or constitutional binding return. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: safety policy enforcement does not assume ownership of the organization&#39;s ultimate identity or mission. - Evidence: permissionmanager.rs; tools.rs; watchdog.rs; mod.rs; mod.rs - Basis: structural -\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/dcee04ec283e9c0cc14102fea8ce86b587e3203a/assessments/zap.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit dcee04ec283e9c0cc14102fea8ce86b587e3203a\">dcee04e &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr data-a=\"1\" data-c=\"1\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-10-26T17:49:00+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/alamops/agetor\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Agetor</a><small>2026</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"1\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>1</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>1</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-10-26\">2026-10-26</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/alamops/agetor/tree/c5cf1a2d431f2891506a26010695289a1b6c46ae\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision c5cf1a2d431f2891506a26010695289a1b6c46ae\">c5cf1a2 &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: Carry out a scoped software task or pipeline-step operation through an active model-backed coding harness and return changed artifacts, answers, test outputs or structured handoff. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Uncertain codebase state, task-specific implementation choices, test failures, unexpected tool results, external dependencies and user follow-up requirements. - Decisive decision or feedback right: A configured Claude/Codex/Cursor/Gemini/fx/Antigravity agent chooses concrete code reads/edits and tool actions within its own native permissions; Agetor allocates task worktree/session, sends prompt/turn, records result and relays further input. - Decision owner: Task&#39;s configured model-backed agent running under an Agetor-authored task/step envelope; the Agetor transport/board alone does not semantically solve the coding task. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Worktree pinned-base isolation, selected harness/profile, native CLI/ACP/tmux driver, per-task run records, status events, user interaction cards and pipeline &lt;handoff&gt; capture. - Closure path: User task/card or pipeline goal → Agetor creates task/worktree and launches selected CLI → model agent selects work/tool operations → resulting source/output/status/handoff is captured → task card or next step/human receives feedback. - Boundary reachability: The normal Run action in README and startTask/startTaskInner connect local task data to real installed provider launch implementations; a pipeline launchStep creates a hidden task and invokes the same running path. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Without executing the selected model-backed CLI, board transitions and tmux/branch scripts cannot autonomously choose the requested source-level implementation, testing or explanation. - Evidence: shipped Run and supported harness contract; task start and agent launch dispatch; provider agent bridge; pipeline hidden step task launch. - Basis: Executable first-party task/harness orchestration with model action/feedback path in standard supported mode. - Confidence: High for reachable task transport, medium for runtime behavior across experimental providers. - Caveats: Actual model, account, macOS-compatible binary and chosen permissions must be present. Source/CI fixtures do not measure quality or safety; no agent execution was performed here.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: — - Function: Would attenuate a specific actual inter-S1 collision and return a coordinated adjustment to the competing workers. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Concurrent workers editing shared files, incompatible assumptions passed between different steps, and competing changes requiring arbitration rather than isolation alone. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Each ordinary card has its own branch/worktree, and pipeline parent state has serialized updates; Agetor provides warnings and human-guided git conflict tools but no source-established autonomous collision-reading mediator between two S1s. - Decision owner: Human project operator or external model agent separately assigned a repair task, not the built-in routing or worktree allocator as an independent S2 model regulator. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Per-task worktrees, per-parent pipeline locks, parallel step list in task prompt, explicit warning to avoid overlapping files, manual merge-conflict repair task, Git status and PR checks. - Closure path: Isolation prevents many cross-card overwrites; within a pipeline, parallel agents share a worktree and receive a warning. An actual source collision is not diagnosed and negotiated by a first-party dedicated S2 arbiter before workers modify their behavior. - Boundary reachability: All named mechanisms are available, but an inter-worker semantic conflict → discretionary settlement → instructions back to the distinct conflicting agents is not evidenced in a standard shipped construction. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Per-card worktree separation and serialized metadata updates are mechanical conflict avoidance, not an owner assessing the incompatibility between two workers and changing their commitments. - Evidence: parallel pipeline worktree collision warning; parent update lock and shared step worktree; parallel worker warning in composed prompt. - Basis: Source-negative for distinct inter-S1 conflict attenuation at this recursion; mechanical safety surfaces are substantial but not S2. - Confidence: Medium-high. - Caveats: A bespoke user-built coordination agent could be added, but unbounded external prompt engineering is not credited as a first-party owned standard mode.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: — - Function: Would own a discretionary, whole-current task/pipeline view and redirect present commitments among operational units based on feedback. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Current mission drift, multi-agent scheduling priorities, failures after review, task-level reallocation and overall acceptance/readiness conflicts. - Decisive decision or feedback right: The user defines the task board and pipeline graph. The pipeline runner auto-follows single edges or fan-out, passes the current step agent&#39;s selected outgoing next to a predefined graph edge, and blocks/moves for explicit human retry/advance on failures. No shipped model PM with whole-current decision rights is evidenced. - Decision owner: Human operator for mission-level choices and a deterministic pipeline engine for prescribed state transitions; individual agents own only their assigned step or local branch suggestion. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Pipeline snapshot, handoff schema, resolveNextSteps, sequential/parallel edges, step caps, once-only malformed-handoff reminder, task status and manual Advance/Retry/Stop actions. - Closure path: User-authored graph → step worker sees overall goal and preceding handoff → worker chooses a permitted next only when multiple outgoing edges exist → deterministic runner launches allowed successor or blocks for user. This is step-local routing, not a dedicated whole-current discretionary control loop. - Boundary reachability: The runner is fully connected, but its own emitted step prompts state that the worker is responsible for its step only, not the whole pipeline. An arbitrary user-authored supervisory prompt is not a shipped, bounded current-manager construction. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Model choice of an allowed outgoing edge is real but remains local operational branch selection; the broader task portfolio priorities, coordination, stop/replan and acceptance still reside with the operator or predefined code. - Evidence: step-scoped authority in composed prompt; single/all/agent-next routing; runner reacts to settled steps; operator retry/advance modes. - Basis: Negative for a whole-current discretionary owner; positive local branching and execution scheduling deliberately not overcounted. - Confidence: Medium-high for published normal pipeline contract. - Caveats: A user could define an entire PM-agent workflow using generic agent profiles; mere capability to author arbitrary instructions does not establish a first-party S3 current-governor path.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-c\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: C - Function: Allow a user-built pipeline to insert a separate model-backed Review role to test a preceding implementer&#39;s changes and return a pass/fail route for correction. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Implementation errors or missing requirements concealed by an implementer&#39;s self-reported success and undiscovered branch regressions. - Decisive decision or feedback right: A separately configured Review agent/profile, when included in a pipeline, runs as its own hidden step task against the same original worktree and parent goal, then may place next: Implement or next: Docs/finish in the handoff according to its inspection verdict. - Decision owner: Distinct user-selected model-backed reviewer step operating through Agetor&#39;s first-party snapshot/launch/return facility; user chooses role/instructions and native CLI permissions. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Separate agent-profile step, code worktree visibility, prior handoff with untrusted-data framing, prompt containing overall goal, structured &lt;handoff&gt; verdict/next target, graph transition restrictions and runner&#39;s follow-up execution. - Closure path: Implementer produces source changes and &lt;handoff&gt; → configured separate Review agent receives goal, previous outcome and same worktree → reviewer examines original files/tests/diff if its profile instructions require this → reviewer selects a configured correction/approval edge → runner re-launches Implement or advances to next stage. - Boundary reachability: README ships an explicit Plan → Implement → Review graph with Review -- changes requested --&gt; Implement and Review -- approved --&gt; Docs. pipeline-runner.ts constructs hidden model-backed Review tasks from selected profiles and passes original worktree; pipeline.ts renders untrusted handoff context and checks route; resolveNextSteps consumes reviewer-selected next when multiple edges exist. The construction is optional, not a built-in always-on independent reviewer. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: The separate model step can make a semantic correctness assessment using the actual project artifacts and redirect work. Graph labels alone, diff UI or the original implementing agent&#39;s self-review would not meet this condition. - Claim being audited: Implementer&#39;s assertion that current branch satisfies the requested software goal and is ready to advance. - Ordinary reporting path: Implementer agent&#39;s final message/structured handoff, task status and ordinary saved transcript. - Complementary access path: Different model-agent step receives original source worktree, goal and earlier handoff (explicitly fenced as untrusted), and can inspect files/tests independently rather than only trusting the implementation summary. - Independence boundary: Distinct selected agent profile and separate hidden step task/turn; same worktree, same user machine and potentially same vendor/model, with no mandatory independent sandbox or read-only reviewer policy. - Who acts on findings: The Agetor runner follows the reviewer&#39;s configured next edge back to the Implement agent when changes are requested; unresolved steps can block and require human choice. - Evidence: first-party graph review/correction example; per-step agent task and worktree; original goal, untrusted prior output and step instructions; model-chosen rework edge. - Basis: Supported but user-assembled independent-review constructor with real source/handoff access and correction-edge return. - Confidence: Medium; no built-in substantive reviewer prompt, required original-artifact checklist or mandatory model separation was established. - Caveats: The user must configure a genuinely separate reviewer profile, original-artifact inspection instruction, and rework edge; arbitrary pipeline called “review” without actual inspection is not S3. Native fx safety tool reviewer is fx-owned and excluded.\">C</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: — - Function: Would explore future external changes and choose enduring new capabilities for the local work organization, returning those to present work. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Future coding agent/provider developments, new methods, product/market conditions or unseen environment changes beyond current task execution. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Agetor discovers model names, available harnesses and account plans, and operators update agents/pipelines; no independent model-backed strategic external sensing and autonomous future adaptation owner was found. - Decision owner: Human Agetor operator chooses agent profiles, models, next tasks, new workflow capabilities and app updates; discovery feeds current CLI compatibility. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: CLI model discovery, version checks, presets, exported agent profiles, pipeline editor, update facility and usage meter. - Closure path: Updated model catalog may enable an operator to choose another harness for a future task, but is not an agent-owned prospective intelligence → adaptation design → current-control return circuit. - Boundary reachability: Model discovery is operationally wired yet only exposes currently available provider options and refusal/upgrade hints; it is not a future capability decision-making layer. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Enumerating available models and remembering configured profiles does not autonomously weigh a future environmental threat and redesign organizational capabilities. - Evidence: model/provider discovery and profile control; harness discovery code; user-owned pipeline configuration. - Basis: Negative for prospective agent-owned capability strategy. - Confidence: Medium-high. - Caveats: An external agent could be directed to research a future platform change as an S1 task; task-level research alone is not a standing S4 regulator of Agetor.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: — - Function: Would decide ultimate legitimate purpose/identity disputes and install binding organizational policy. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Contradictory ultimate goals, stakeholder values or legitimate final authority above task/CLI permissions and PR approvals. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Agetor replays native provider approval questions, exposes per-agent safety modes and lets the user approve diff/PR delivery. It does not decide ultimate purpose or define a constitutional hierarchy. - Decision owner: User/operator of the local workspace, and native external provider permissions for individual tools; application itself is a bounded workflow interface. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Session permission cards, native CLI modes, worktree restrictions, Git host authentication and human PR publication/merge choices. - Closure path: A user resolves an external approval question or changes a harness mode, which changes allowed operations in the current task; no identity-conflict decision is made and returned as enduring purpose policy. - Boundary reachability: Approval UI and provider-native mode controls exist and can be exercised, but no first-party ultimate-policy judgment owner is reached. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Existing user privileges and native CLI sandbox decisions are fixed conditions and operator choices, not autonomous adjudication of the organization&#39;s higher purpose. - Evidence: permission and approval interface; explicit native privilege warning; user-controlled pipeline and PR actions. - Basis: Bounded source-negative for identity and ultimate policy. - Confidence: High at the chosen task-workbench recursion. - Caveats: Workspace human may be a parent S5 for its wider business, but the user providing permissions or reviewing a PR is not automatically a first-party Agetor P mode.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/63f8632adc972ee5de22e2e624835305aaf7bf68/assessments/ag/agetor.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit 63f8632adc972ee5de22e2e624835305aaf7bf68\">63f8632 &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr data-a=\"1\" data-c=\"0\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-10-19T23:49:47+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/yoheinakajima/activegraph\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Active Graph</a><small>2026</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"1\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>1</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>0</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-10-19\">2026-10-19</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/yoheinakajima/activegraph/tree/50d8405923b79e4e823a73a3045bb969436a872c\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision 50d8405923b79e4e823a73a3045bb969436a872c\">50d8405 &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: Execute context-sensitive agent steps against a changing graph/goal, using model-selected responses, tools and observed results in the supported live-provider mode. - Disturbance / variety regulated: New goal types, variable evidence and graph events, differing tool result content, tool/model failures and uncertain model next actions. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Registered @llmbehavior provider model selects a response or declared tool call from the current graph view, tool definitions and prior tool messages; output handler turns model results into task-relevant graph mutations and reactive follow-up. - Decision owner: Configured LLM through first-party LLMBehavior, Runtime.invokellm, LLMProvider.complete and tool dispatch; the provider infrastructure is external, but the shipped model-decision composition is implemented here. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Event subscriptions, contextual graph view, frame, policy, tools registry, behavior scheduling, accepted patches, recorded event causality, tool-turn bounds and tool-error feedback. - Closure path: Goal/event → model-backed behavior → provider completion → selected tool(s) executed → result messages re-enter same model loop → final interpreted output and proposed graph patch → event-sourced graph updated → next event triggers model/deterministic behavior. - Boundary reachability: Public @llmbehavior and Runtime(graph,llmprovider=...) directly enter runtime-owned invokellm → live provider call/replay → tool dispatch → result to subsequent LLM turn → BehaviorGraph proposal. The bundled Diligence pack includes executable model-backed behavior definitions; the offline quickstart exercises identical event/behavior composition with a deliberately recorded surrogate. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: If the model actor is removed but the event router, graph store, tool registry and policy remain, deterministic subscribers can fire but cannot generate varied task-sensitive decisions and tools from unknown evidence. Recorded fixtures are not live cognition; opt-in shipped provider mode is the S1 positive. - Evidence: activegraph/behaviors/decorators.py#L215, activegraph/runtime/runtime.py#L1050, activegraph/runtime/runtime.py#L1293, activegraph/runtime/runtime.py#L1481, activegraph/runtime/runtime.py#L1903, activegraph/runtime/runtime.py#L1979, activegraph/runtime/runtime.py#L2097, activegraph/packs/diligence/behaviors.py#L111. - Basis: Structural first-party model-provider/tool feedback route with supported optional live mode, plus actual recorded-fixture offline quickstart. - Confidence: High for source reachability and offline execution; model service reliability unverified. - Caveats: Optional external provider SDK/API is needed for real LLM decisions; offline fixture success verifies the orchestrating mechanics only, not model performance.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: — - Function: No distinct first-party owner of regulation of specific disturbances between independently viable S1 operating divisions with mutually revised commitments is evidenced at the declared generic graph-runtime recursion. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Concurrent graph patches, relation-edge changes, conflicting object updates and event-trigger order can interfere with graph consistency; those disturbances are addressed by optimistic consistency/rejection and queued deterministic handling, not an evidenced portfolio of separately viable S1 operations. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Registered relation behaviors and graph-event selectors apply user-authored transformations; runtime queues, patch conflict rejection and serialized updates resolve graph state validity. No source-backed independent S1 units plus separate feedback-regulating owner are specified. - Decision owner: Developer-defined relation/regular behavior code and deterministic runtime patch/queue rules; any optional model-backed relation would need concrete independent-S1 ownership evidence beyond this general API. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Typed relations and reactive handlers, event subscription/patterns, optimistic patch validation, deterministic event queue, bounded scheduling and runtime commit log. - Closure path: Event/patch contention → deterministic commit/reject → subsequent event handlers read revised graph; this is shared-state coherence, not inter-S1 operational interference → S2 decision → adjusted independent organizational commitments. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Removing a hypothetical coordinating model still leaves the same generic queue/patch-rejection machinery. A programmable relation handler is a possible application construction site, not a proven default S2 autonomously exercising relational coordination. - Evidence: activegraph/behaviors/decorators.py#L150, activegraph/runtime/runtime.py#L1072, activegraph/runtime/runtime.py#L1293, activegraph/runtime/authority.py#L103, activegraph/core/patch.py#L1, activegraph/core/graph.py#L1. - Basis: Bounded negative in first-party runtime and bundled example, not universal claim about all third-party packs. - Confidence: Medium-high. - Caveats: A separate application may configure real independent S1 divisions and bind a model-led relation coordinator, requiring a new system boundary and evidence.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: — - Function: No first-party autonomous whole-current manager controls independent viable operating divisions&#39; resources, priorities and obligations with a managerial return path. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Event queue or LLM/tool budget exhaustion, failed behaviors, sandbox constraints and approval waiting can threaten a graph run, but these are local run-level risks. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Developer supplies Frame, Policy, budget limits, tools and registered behaviors; first-party executor checks them, schedules ready behaviors and emits failure/budget events. No separate agent is shown renegotiating a portfolio of independent S1 commitments. - Decision owner: Human/application constructor and deterministic runtime budget/scheduler/authority evaluator, not an operational S3 executive. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Frame goal/constraints, budget consumption and runquantum, scheduler, policy capability ceilings, approval and failure events, event sink status. - Closure path: Run condition → hard-coded/configured guard or scheduling choice → current behavior invoked/stopped/failure logged; missing whole-current viability manager → new binding priorities/resource rights returned to multiple S1 units. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Budget guards and queues are safety/throughput controls; removing a hypothetical model S3 still leaves all their fixed effects intact. - Evidence: activegraph/frame.py#L1, activegraph/policy.py#L1, activegraph/runtime/authority.py#L55, activegraph/runtime/runtime.py#L1072, activegraph/runtime/runtime.py#L1364. - Basis: Bounded first-party negative including provider-configured model behavior, deterministic and pack modes. - Confidence: Medium-high. - Caveats: Downstream application may manage many viable S1 systems atop this runtime; generic registered behavior count is not itself that scope.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: — - Function: No separate first-party complementary reality-inspection function independently challenges a current S3 manager&#39;s operating picture and returns a corrective action to that manager. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Incomplete causal traces, untrusted tool/model outputs and behavior faults can mislead users. Event logs, strict replay, fork/diff and observability expose inconsistencies but do not themselves close managerial audit. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Replay divergence checks and error events can flag mismatched execution; an operator chooses what to inspect. No distinct S3 agent samples actual S1 reality, judges S3 reports and sends an authorized correction into separate S3 decision rights. - Decision owner: First-party deterministic trace/replay machinery and outside human/developer for interpretation; no autonomous audit owner at this recursion. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Append-only event log, causal provenance, strict replay, tracing/metrics/sinks, patch rejection events and fork/diff. - Closure path: Events → trace or replay discrepancy → user inspection/failure event; no independent operational audit finding → established S3 receives it → changes current S1 portfolio control. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Full replayability is valuable evidence infrastructure but removing a putative auditor does not change the deterministic event-history artifact. Evidence capture is not an independent audit decision/closure by itself. - Evidence: activegraph/runtime/runtime.py#L3393, activegraph/runtime/runtime.py#L3583, activegraph/runtime/diff.py#L108, activegraph/trace/causal.py#L1, activegraph/observability/status.py#L1. - Basis: Bounded negative after checking dedicated observability and strict replay capabilities. - Confidence: Medium-high. - Caveats: Users may implement a qualified external reviewer or application-specific reality-checking model; no such legitimate S3-to-S3 relation is bundled.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: — - Function: No first-party model-owned future environment distinction → prospective changed strategy option → authorized durable adoption back into later operations cycle is established by the shipped event-sourcing/replay/fork mechanisms. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Future model/prompt/graph configuration hypotheses, possible failure recurrence and drift can be explored with forks; the choices of what to test and retain remain application/developer-owned. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Runtime.fork creates a branch from recorded history, diff compares structural outcomes, and replay cache preserves event/model responses. The caller determines alternative behaviors/policies and whether/how to deploy them; no packaged model evaluates environmental future shifts and autonomously updates durable operating capabilities. - Decision owner: Outside experimenter/developer for prospective configuration; deterministic runtime for producing comparable fork traces. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Replay cache, fork IDs, Diff computation, consistent event roots, saved packs/behaviors and optional user-supplied configuration. - Closure path: Operator forks run → changes input configuration → replay and diff → operator interprets result; no internal model-generated prospective strategy with authorized adoption into next live baseline operating mode. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: A future developer can use the instrumentation for an S4 experiment, but removing any supposed strategic model does not disable graph replay, fork or diff; no model-owned future option-generation-and-adoption path is first-party evidenced. - Evidence: activegraph/runtime/runtime.py#L3393, activegraph/runtime/runtime.py#L3583, activegraph/runtime/diff.py#L79, activegraph/llm/cache.py#L1, README.md#L42. - Basis: Bounded negative even though deterministic experimentation infrastructure is strong. - Confidence: Medium-high at the declared generic runtime boundary. - Caveats: A user-created LLM behavior might intentionally analyze trends and update a future skill; that is application-specific S4, not shipped default first-party ownership.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: — - Function: No first-party legitimate ultimate identity/purpose authority deciding constitutional conflicts with a lasting policy return is evidenced. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Tool capabilities, graph mutation scope, replay security, sandbox access and human approvals bound model behavior but do not define organization-level identity decisions. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Policy/frame ceilings and evaluateactionauthority enforce user-supplied permissions; explicit operator approval can control capabilities, but no internally accountable actor deliberates and sets final purpose for a viable organization. - Decision owner: External developer/operator supplies governance intent; first-party deterministic permission/approval enforcement. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: AuthorityDecision, capability ceilings and policy configuration, patch validation, approval events, sandbox and budget locks. - Closure path: Tool/patch request → predetermined policy or outside approval → action allowed/denied; no identity-question escalation to legitimate S5 and durable return of constitutional policy. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: The string Policy describes an enforceable rule set, not organizational legitimacy or the right to decide ultimate purpose. - Evidence: activegraph/policy.py#L1, activegraph/runtime/authority.py#L55, activegraph/runtime/authority.py#L103, activegraph/frame.py#L1, README.md#L150. - Basis: Bounded negative after first-party frame/authority and human approval modes. - Confidence: Medium-high. - Caveats: The human/application owning a higher-level organization may exercise S5 externally; not claimed inside this runtime.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/3f3f65314f63d2b23f5de0b1c2512d780daee008/assessments/ac/active-graph.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit 3f3f65314f63d2b23f5de0b1c2512d780daee008\">3f3f653 &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr data-a=\"1\" data-c=\"0\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-10-10T17:45:03+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/Hash-7777/HashCortX\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">HashCortX</a><small>2026</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"1\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>1</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>0</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-10-10\">2026-10-10</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/Hash-7777/HashCortX/tree/ab3fb74a61928dfb98985eac5ec3eeac8c85afe7\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision ab3fb74a61928dfb98985eac5ec3eeac8c85afe7\">ab3fb74 &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: A single autonomous model-directed coding loop acts on user project files and tools. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Changing code, failing commands, requested edits and tool errors. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Select files/commands/edits, observe first-party tool results and determine the next coding action. - Decision owner: Main model-driven HashCoder agent. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Native Tauri/Rust tool adapters, permission gates, model routing, session persistence, project scope and diff/undo. - Closure path: User task -&gt; model-chosen coding tool -&gt; first-party handler and tool feedback -&gt; new model choice or final result. - Boundary reachability: HashCoder&#39;s shipped code mode calls its native agentLoop and Tauri tool definitions on a real opened project. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: The model chooses actions; the program executes and records those choices. - Evidence: mode.js; hashcoder.js; agent-policy.js - Basis: structural + explicit. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: Inference comes from configured external models; permissions and human approval can constrain actions..\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: — - Function: No established first-party inter-S1 regulation inside the HashCoder coding runtime. - Disturbance / variety regulated: A single coding agent and its tool batch do not form distinct operational units in conflict. - Decisive decision or feedback right: No S2-specific choice. - Decision owner: No S2 decision owner established. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Concurrent read batching, sequential writes, session and UI state. - Closure path: Tools are grouped and executed for the same operational agent, not corrective coordination among independent S1 cells. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Parallel read tools and sequential writes only implement one agent&#39;s tool ordering; the separate Swarm workspace is excluded. - Evidence: agent-policy.js; mode.js; graph.js; ARCHITECTURE.md - Basis: structural + explicit. - Confidence: medium-high. - Caveats: Sibling Swarm team design is an adjacent system and cannot donate its decisions to HashCoder..\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: — - Function: No distinct whole-system current management over multiple coding operational units. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Iteration stalls, limits and tool permission issues are local operating disturbances. - Decisive decision or feedback right: None for organizational resource/commitment authority above one coding S1. - Decision owner: No qualifying S3 owner. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Stall detector, step budgets, runtime gate, provider fallback, status and trace. - Closure path: Stalled or unproven work is returned to the same coding agent or stopped; no whole-current cross-unit regulation. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Deterministic current action limits are enforcement mechanisms, not a metasystem choosing shared unit priorities. - Evidence: agent-policy.js; mode.js; verify.js - Basis: structural + explicit. - Confidence: medium-high. - Caveats: These protections may improve performance/safety but do not establish organizational S3..\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: — - Function: No sufficiently independent audit role with complementary evidence access and corrective organizational return. - Disturbance / variety regulated: A coding agent may prematurely claim correct changes. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Proof tracker evaluates recorded test commands and deterministic send-back conditions, not independent review judgment. - Decision owner: Main agent retains correction ownership; proof checker applies static record checks. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Project test command discovery, proofLog, change timestamps and bounded completion prompts. - Closure path: Recorded same-loop tool output -&gt; deterministic nudge -&gt; same model executes or answers; no separate auditor. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: The evidence is generated from ordinary agent operations; separate audit access/owner is missing. - Evidence: verify.js; mode.js; agent-policy.js - Basis: structural + explicit. - Confidence: medium-high. - Caveats: Proof log can honestly describe which tests ran, without becoming S3..\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: — - Function: No first-party prospective external-environment adaptation loop in the credited HashCoder mode. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Future capability demands and external changes are not explicitly converted into adaptation options. - Decisive decision or feedback right: No evidenced S4 capability renewal right. - Decision owner: No S4 agent, constructor or parent owner. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Automatic user-fact extraction, memory recall, model choice, persisted state and tools. - Closure path: Facts may affect later prompts but no prospective environmental intelligence decision changes the current operating organization. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Context retention and provider fallback are not prospective organizational adaptation. - Evidence: store.js; mode.js; ARCHITECTURE.md - Basis: structural + explicit. - Confidence: medium. - Caveats: Other desktop workspaces are separately scoped and cannot close S4 here..\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: — - Function: No first-party organizational identity or ultimate-policy governance loop. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Tool permission/safety choices concern individual operations. - Decisive decision or feedback right: No ultimate-policy decision right at the selected recursion. - Decision owner: Human grants individual permissions; deterministic rules enforce. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Permission Guard, Rust deny list, user action dialog, diff Keep/Undo, audit log. - Closure path: User approves or denies an operation, without a binding identity/ultimate-policy decision loop returned to the organization. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Command authorization and selected model are not S5-level ultimate purpose. - Evidence: hashcoder.js; agentsandbox.rs; SECURITY.md - Basis: structural + explicit. - Confidence: medium-high. - Caveats: Runtime control is not inherited from broader OSS development governance..\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/4ed17d3bae037310dcd91af45f247ffb34cbe94e/assessments/hashcortx.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit 4ed17d3bae037310dcd91af45f247ffb34cbe94e\">4ed17d3 &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr data-a=\"1\" data-c=\"0\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-11-02T21:17:39+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/patw/Pengy\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Pengy</a><small>2026</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"1\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>1</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>0</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-11-02\">2026-11-02</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/patw/Pengy/tree/99b22849176c98ba8f5414edd8817b87cb2fea78\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision 99b22849176c98ba8f5414edd8817b87cb2fea78\">99b2284 &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: Iteratively choose and execute user-relevant tools based on results. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Evolving user requests, environmental observations, tool failures, context limits and interrupted interactions. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Select next action/tool call or final answer after tool results are observed. - Decision owner: The model acting inside the shipped Pengy chat loop. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: OpenAI-compatible client, JSON-schema tools and dispatcher, confirmation modes, context recovery and persistent per-chat histories. - Closure path: Chat context → model response/tool call → local execution/approval → tool result appended → new model turn or completed reply. - Boundary reachability: The CLI/Web/Qt entry points all invoke the same shipped LLMClient.chat generator; ordinary user/provider configuration completes the normal deployment. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: The model chooses substantive operational actions, while the user may deny a tool and the deterministic runtime handles transport and retries. - Evidence: core chat/tool loop, tool registry/execution, CLI entry, README operating modes. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: No actual model API call or end-to-end hosted deployment was reproduced in this review.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: — - Function: No distinct inter-S1 interference attenuation loop is shipped at this boundary. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Potential conflicts among independently operating task units. - Decisive decision or feedback right: None for organization-level coordination. - Decision owner: No qualifying S2 owner established. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Multiple chats/UI tabs, serialized per-chat tool turns, task prompt templates and filesystem locks. - Closure path: No S2-specific returned coordination outcome; each user chat remains a separate S1 interaction. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: UI multiplicity, sequential tool calls and shared local files do not evidence autonomously coordinated S1 units with a conflict/oscillation feedback loop. - Evidence: chat storage and index locking, prompt-template tasks, agent core. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: Custom external multi-agent orchestration is outside the reviewed distribution.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: — - Function: No separately owned whole-system current-control loop is supplied. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Potential workload/capacity/priority disturbances across an operational whole. - Decisive decision or feedback right: None beyond single-session tool choices and user approval. - Decision owner: No qualifying S3 actor or parent feedback loop established. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Tool approval, cancel/retry, context pressure, error events, local UI task controls and prompt-template CRUD. - Closure path: Local tool/turn control returns to S1; no whole-system manager regulates shared current commitments. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Human tool confirmation and deterministic cancellation do not amount to parent current-control of an organization; task templates are not a current workload manager. - Evidence: LLMClient confirmation and recovery, task manager, context recovery. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: An operator may terminate a task, but no S3 parent mode is inferred from that alone.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: — - Function: No independent complementary reality-check and returned correction mechanism is shipped in the standard agent mode. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Potential false claims of correctness after a tool action. - Decisive decision or feedback right: No independent audit judgment over the operational output. - Decision owner: No qualifying audit actor. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Tool confirmations, diff previews, app tests and optional read-only checks expressed in user-authored prompt templates. - Closure path: Ordinary tool results and user feedback return to S1; no separate S3 findings-to-correction loop is established. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Developer tests and confirmation/diff UI do not create a complementary agent or independent audit judgment with corrective feedback. - Evidence: tool execution/confirmation, core chat, task templates. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: medium. - Caveats: Users could install a third-party reviewer skill, but no first-party independent corrective loop is credited from hypothetical composition.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: — - Function: No prospective external environment-to-capability adaptation decision path is supplied. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Long-run mismatch between available capability and changing environment. - Decisive decision or feedback right: None for new future operating strategy or capability selection. - Decision owner: No qualifying S4 owner identified. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Context summaries, memory-skill CRUD/search, local session history, user-selected models and optional skill installation. - Closure path: Past context and recalled notes may affect ordinary next S1 turns, not a prospective adaptation program. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Persistence, summarization, local memory, model preferences and user-managed skill import do not themselves select/exercise externally informed future capability change. - Evidence: context recovery, memory skill, skill install. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: A user can manually alter installed tools; that is not evidence of a first-party S4 adaptation loop.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: — - Function: No live identity/ultimate-policy arbitration and return path is supplied. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Possible disagreement over the assistant&#39;s ultimate purpose/legitimate authority. - Decisive decision or feedback right: No first-party ultimate-policy decision process; static user/system configuration constrains task execution. - Decision owner: Ordinary user/configuration choices; no evidenced S5 function at the declared recursion. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Local settings, model selection, system message, approval mode, skill pointers and safe tool constraints. - Closure path: Configuration is read into a session; no function-specific identity dispute → legitimate authority → returned governing decision is observed. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Tool permissions and user preference edits control S1 behavior; they do not independently establish identity-policy adjudication. - Evidence: configuration, LLMClient tool confirmation, README. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: The open-source project&#39;s own governance and external provider terms are not credited as runtime S5.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/20f818749e785b1fa4ab2933835bff57b9f7c270/assessments/pe/pengy.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit 20f818749e785b1fa4ab2933835bff57b9f7c270\">20f8187 &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr data-a=\"1\" data-c=\"0\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-10-12T03:03:18+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/nattergabriel/termcoder\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Termcoder</a><small>2026</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"1\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>1</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>0</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-10-12\">2026-10-12</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/nattergabriel/termcoder/tree/c642c36e8d65ca42a8ae05172c527f73cb2017d7\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision c642c36e8d65ca42a8ae05172c527f73cb2017d7\">c642c36 &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: carry out uncertain coding/developer tasks through chosen code-inspection, search, editing and shell operations informed by real tool feedback. - Disturbance / variety regulated: unknown project structure, unavailable files, code defects, command failure, unexpected results and subsequent needed steps. - Decisive decision or feedback right: model chooses a sequence of typed tools and the follow-up or terminal response based on previous tool outputs. - Decision owner: model-driven first-party Agent.runturn workflow. Provider supplies model inference but the installed app binds tools, executes actions and supplies feedback. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Registry, provider adapter, State, tool implementations, permission callbacks, channel UI, config/skills, iteration cap and rollback. - Closure path: user task → model tool request → permission decision → real registered tool execution → ToolResult appended as model context → model chooses next action or completes turn. - Boundary reachability: app.build supplies the native registry/provider/permission callback to Agent, and the supported terminal/Telegram modes run that same first-party loop. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: removing model choice leaves a deterministic tool list and permission prompts but no context-sensitive coding plan, inspection or completion decision. - Evidence: agent loop; app composition; tools; integration tests. - Basis: structural and explicit. - Confidence: high for implementation-defined tool feedback; live provider-backed task not reproduced. - Caveats: mutating calls remain subject to human permission, and external providers remain dependencies.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: — - Function: no autonomous or constructor-owned first-party regulation of specific interference/oscillation between distinct operating S1 units is evidenced. - Disturbance / variety regulated: multiple tool calls and incoming prompts are handled within one agent&#39;s own sequence, rather than competing independently goal-directed agent workers. - Decisive decision or feedback right: serial dispatchall runs model-requested tools; UI input queue transports future user messages without conflict arbitration between autonomous S1s. - Decision owner: model for its own operation, runtime for serial dispatch, channel for input handling; no separate S2 closure. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: tool registry, sequential dispatch, Telegram input queue, slash-command/user channel routing. - Closure path: result of each tool feeds the same model&#39;s next inference. No inter-S1 conflict signal → specific attenuation decision → changed subsequent worker obligations. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: sequencing one operative&#39;s tools or swapping communications channels is not the S2 functional relation. - Evidence: loop.py; telegram.py; app.py. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: high within shipped single-agent modes. - Caveats: independent user-launched termcoder processes could interfere externally without creating native cross-process coordination.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: — - Function: no whole-current management function over a population of operating S1 units with authority over shared resources/commitments is instantiated. - Disturbance / variety regulated: current task and conversation progress are local to the only S1, not a managed organization&#39;s distinct current commitments. - Decisive decision or feedback right: coding model decides its own tool sequence; channel/user can approve or refuse operations; no whole-system manager allocates resources, changes priority across workers or returns corrected commitments. - Decision owner: the active coding model, human permissions/configuration and deterministic runtime; no separate S3 owner. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: iteration limit, provider/model commands, task-local message state, approval gate. - Closure path: task feedback and user guidance directly return to the operative model; no distinct whole-current view → corrective S3 decision → multiple S1 adjustment. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: present coding-task management is S1 even when the model can change models or adjust its next action. - Evidence: app.py; loop.py; model command; config. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: high for standard application boundary. - Caveats: external human operating several instances is not first-party returned S3 governance.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: — - Function: no sufficiently independent reality-sampling auditor emits a separate judgment and closes a feedback path into S3 management. - Disturbance / variety regulated: tool/test failures can be seen by the active coding model and tests can be run as tools, but these are ordinary execution feedback rather than independent operational auditing. - Decisive decision or feedback right: model may invoke tests/shell or view error messages; repo integration tests run by external developers/CI. None constitutes a first-party independent audit decision and management return. - Decision owner: operative coding model for tool-followup, developer/CI for separate quality tests; no S3 audit owner in the deployed agent. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: shell result, tool error marker, iterative model reasoning, developer tests. - Closure path: failed tool or test output → same agent&#39;s subsequent action; no independent complementary auditor → managerial owner → binding corrective instruction. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: routine self-verification and off-runtime test suites do not establish the S3 function. - Evidence: loop.py; Bash tool; loop test; development CI. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: medium-high. - Caveats: human review or a downstream plugin could introduce an audit, but no such shipped ownership loop was found.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: — - Function: no first-party closed future/environment-sensing adaptation function is shipped to alter sustained organizational capability. - Disturbance / variety regulated: changing provider/models and source content may influence today&#39;s coding task; there is no prospective adaptation program. - Decisive decision or feedback right: user selects/configures provider, model and skills; a model may adapt local tactics after tool feedback, not authorize a future-oriented change in organizational capability. - Decision owner: user/developer for configuration and skill provision, current task model for immediate work. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: provider registry, slash commands, global/project config, discovered Agent Skills and project instructions. - Closure path: user changes settings → next local session/tool choices; no distinct outside/future observation → capability proposal → S4 decision → returned organizational adaptation. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: task-local reasoning, model switching and skill activation are not substantive external-and-prospective S4 by themselves. - Evidence: config.py; app.py; SkillCatalog; provider command. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: high on bounded standard modes. - Caveats: custom extensions might implement a separate adaptation function, not attributed to standard distribution.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: — - Function: no first-party legitimate ultimate-policy or continuing-identity arbitration loop at the agent-organization boundary. - Disturbance / variety regulated: permission postures, project instructions and credentials govern local tool execution, not organization identity. - Decisive decision or feedback right: human approval and static policy choose whether particular tool calls are allowed. No S5 identity decision is made and returned as binding whole-system governance. - Decision owner: user/configuration for permissions, deterministic runtime for enforcement; no S5 decisive owner. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: askeach, allowreadonly, allowall, project/user config and tool metadata. - Closure path: user/local config → permission callback → tool allowed/denied. No legitimate ultimate authority receives an S5 issue and returns an organizational identity-level decision. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: a permission gate or a system prompt cannot be credited as ultimate policy/identity decision ownership without the function-specific closure. - Evidence: permissions.py; config.py; app.py; prompt. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: high within shipped modes. - Caveats: project maintainer governance occurs outside the packaged agent&#39;s operational recursion.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/e953e47c78813c4c7796ae402f875b38e866a890/assessments/termcoder.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit e953e47c78813c4c7796ae402f875b38e866a890\">e953e47 &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr data-a=\"1\" data-c=\"0\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-09-24T19:38:32Z\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/Airavat-Research/cascaide-ts\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">cascaide-ts</a><small>2026</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"1\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>1</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>0</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-09-24\">2026-09-24</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/Airavat-Research/cascaide-ts/tree/08fc10fcd58ecf729b9b0c5c1f0c135f7bb723f7\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision 08fc10fcd58ecf729b9b0c5c1f0c135f7bb723f7\">08fc10f &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: perform goal-directed agent work through repeated model decisions, tool/delegation actions and returned observations within a Cascaide cascade. - Disturbance / variety regulated: user/application requests, evolving conversation history, model uncertainty, tool/sub-agent outputs and errors, provider responses and the local state needed to decide whether to act again or complete. - Decisive decision or feedback right: choose the next substantive answer, direct tool call, self-delegation or named-sub-agent delegation from the current model-visible context and incorporate returned results into the next turn. - Decision owner: the model-driven agent actor invoked through the first-party helper-built agent node. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: core node lifecycle, workflow context, provider adapters, tool-node execution, child-cascade spawning/waiting, recursion depth guard, streaming normalization, active-node tracking, optional persistence/hydration and client/server relay. - Closure path: cascade input/history → helper agent node → provider-backed model decision → answer or Tool/delegation call → first-party tool node executes the selected action/subcascade → tool result is appended to cascade history → helper respawns the agent node → model receives the observation and decides again or terminates. - Boundary reachability: @cascaide-ts/helpers is a shipped package explicitly documented as providing createReactAgent, createRecursiveReactAgent, createSupervisorAgent and createRecursiveSupervisorAgent; those generated nodes execute directly through the documented @cascaide-ts/core engine rather than through a repository-development example path. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Cascaide&#39;s deterministic graph executor transports, persists and bounds the loop, while the model actor chooses substantive next actions from the current history and declared capabilities. External providers supply inference but are directly invoked as the actor in the first-party operating path. - Evidence: createAgent.ts; serverWorkflowListenerMiddleware.ts; packages/helpers/README.md. - Basis: explicit + structural. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: the application chooses the model, system prompt, direct tools and optional sub-agent catalog. Those constructor inputs bound S1 but do not move the turn-level action decision out of the model actor once the standard helper runtime is executing.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: — - Function: no material first-party S2 coordination function is established at the declared cascade recursion. - Disturbance / variety regulated: parallel or delegated S1 units could in principle overlap, conflict or oscillate, but the reviewed standard distribution does not identify a concrete sibling-interference mode and close a response specifically intended to damp it. - Decisive decision or feedback right: none established for inter-S1 conflict/oscillation attenuation. - Decision owner: none established. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: delegateToSubAgents() can spawn several child cascades and wait for them; recursive delegation is described for independent subtasks; activeNodes tracks concurrent work; persistence claims prevent duplicate execution of a node instance. - Closure path: no first-party disturbance-specific coordination judgment was found that returns a conflict/oscillation attenuation decision into subsequent behavior of multiple S1 units. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: the parent model may choose subtasks or named sub-agents, but delegation and task decomposition are not S2 by themselves. spawn, waitUntil, active-node bookkeeping and node claiming transport/order execution without selecting a coordination response to a evidenced inter-S1 disturbance. - Evidence: createAgent.ts; controller.ts; serverPersistenceMiddleware.ts. - Basis: explicit + structural absence. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: downstream graph/node code could use the generic controller to implement a domain-specific coordination mechanism, but a universal extension surface is not itself an S2 constructor path without function-specific interference and closure evidence.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: — - Function: no material first-party whole-cascade current-control function with agent-owned authority is established. - Disturbance / variety regulated: current whole-cascade commitments, competing priorities, resource constraints or local-vs-global operational exceptions would require a metasystemic current view and intervention right, but the packaged agent paths do not provide that relation. - Decisive decision or feedback right: none established over current resources, commitments, priorities or constraints for the cascade as a whole. - Decision owner: none established. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: WorkflowState exposes context/active nodes/history/errors; CascadeController can read state, spawn nodes, wait and fork; the Supervisor model can delegate to configured sub-agents and receives their completed outputs as tool results; execution-time buffering can checkpoint remaining graph work. - Closure path: no packaged path was found in which an agent receives a whole-cascade current operational view, chooses a current-control intervention over the organization as a whole and returns that decision into runtime state. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Supervisor delegation remains task allocation. The standard Supervisor receives completed child results, not a first-party live whole-system current-control view. Generic controller state/read/write methods are handed to node/tool implementation code and can support downstream specialization, but they do not define or own an S3-specific decision right. - Evidence: createAgent.ts; controller.ts; workflowSlice.ts; serverWorkflowListenerMiddleware.ts. - Basis: explicit + structural absence. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: an application may deliberately expose controller state and intervention tools to a model or human manager, but that would be a separate downstream organization requiring its own evidence rather than an inherited S3 state for the reusable base runtime.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: — - Function: no material first-party complementary audit function is established. - Disturbance / variety regulated: operational claims could require evidence beyond ordinary agent output/history, but the standard runtime does not add an independent challenge path over those claims. - Decisive decision or feedback right: none established for independent audit judgment. - Decision owner: none established. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: durable context events, node history/errors, full audit trails described for durable mode, provider-stream canonicalization, cascade hydration and fork/time-travel. - Closure path: no first-party path was found from complementary evidence or independent challenge → audit finding → downstream corrective current control. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: persistence and replay improve observability and reconstructability, but they record the ordinary execution path. Forking a prior state permits alternative execution but supplies no independent auditor, distinct evidence channel or corrective audit authority by itself. - Evidence: serverPersistenceMiddleware.ts; serverHydrationMiddleware.ts; persistence.ts; README.md. - Basis: explicit + structural absence. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: downstream applications can build reviewers or validators as ordinary graph nodes, but no packaged independent audit relation with complementary access and corrective closure is owned by the reusable runtime.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: — - Function: no material first-party outside-and-future adaptation function is established. - Disturbance / variety regulated: changing external conditions, opportunities, threats or future capability needs are not modeled by a packaged prospective adaptation loop. - Decisive decision or feedback right: none established for generating and selecting future-oriented adaptation options from external distinctions. - Decision owner: none established. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: cascade history/context persistence, hydration, forking/time travel, recursive delegation and application-definable graph transitions. - Closure path: no first-party external/future sensing → adaptation-option generation → return into current capability/control path was found. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: durable state and forkable histories concern preservation/replay of current or past execution. Recursive delegation and graph evolution during a task remain operational/current regulation unless evidence establishes prospective environmental modeling and capability adaptation. - Evidence: serverHydrationMiddleware.ts; controller.ts; workflowSlice.ts. - Basis: explicit + structural absence. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: the generic graph can host research/adaptation agents supplied by an application, but those are downstream semantics rather than a first-party S4 constructor relation in the reviewed base distribution.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: — - Function: no material first-party identity/ultimate-policy closure is established at the cascade recursion. - Disturbance / variety regulated: identity-level conflicts or unresolved S3–S4 policy tensions have no packaged escalation/decision path in the reusable runtime. - Decisive decision or feedback right: none established for legitimate ultimate-policy or identity decisions. - Decision owner: none established. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: application-provided systemPrompt, model/tool/sub-agent configuration, UI-node/HITL construction capability, provider filters and generic graph controls. - Closure path: no identity/policy issue → legitimate ultimate authority → decision → subsequent-operation governance path was found. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: prompts and configuration constrain behavior but do not themselves exercise runtime identity authority. Human interaction can be implemented as a client/UI node, including in scaffolded applications, but generic task-level HITL does not establish S5 and the reusable base does not define an ultimate-policy escalation relation. - Evidence: packages/helpers/src/agent/types.ts; clientWorkflowListenerMiddleware.ts; README.md. - Basis: explicit + structural absence. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: a downstream application may define identity/policy-specific human or agent nodes. Such a specialization is a separate system-in-focus and cannot donate S5 to the reusable Cascaide constructor/runtime.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/1a87050bf4e179a45f42eaca88d2515082fdac2a/assessments/cascaide-ts.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit 1a87050bf4e179a45f42eaca88d2515082fdac2a\">1a87050 &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr data-a=\"1\" data-c=\"2\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-10-01T21:45:40Z\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/skyblanket/swarm-code\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">swarm-code</a><small>2026</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"1\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>1</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>2</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-09-30\">2026-09-30</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/skyblanket/swarm-code/tree/53007abac1d521e32c2508af5328412bd6bbb67b\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision 53007abac1d521e32c2508af5328412bd6bbb67b\">53007ab &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: perform environment-facing coding work by interpreting a user/task objective, selecting repository/tool actions, executing them and revising later action from returned evidence. - Disturbance / variety regulated: repository state, implementation alternatives, shell/file/network/tool results, provider uncertainty, context pressure, tool failures and changing task evidence. - Decisive decision or feedback right: choose the next task-specific tool action and revise that choice from returned tool/model evidence. - Decision owner: the model-backed swarm-code Agent in an interactive/headless parent or /flows child process. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: structured tool-call loop; ToolExecutor; ToolRegistry; permissions/guardrails; sessions/context compaction; skills/MCP; provider adapters; crash recovery; step bounds. - Closure path: current task/history → model selects tool/action → first-party executor performs/denies it → result is appended to history → same model actor chooses another action or final answer. - Boundary reachability: the shipped interactive and headless entrypoints instantiate this loop directly; /flows launches the same product binary as subordinate S1 processes. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: removing the model actor while retaining the executor, sessions and tool registry leaves enforcement machinery but removes the open-ended task-specific decision of what coding action to take next. - Evidence: src/agent.sw; README.md. - Basis: explicit + structural. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: headless and dangerous actions remain constrained by first-party permission/hard-deny policy; those constraints do not replace the model&#39;s ordinary coding discretion.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-c\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: C - Function: attenuate runtime-resource interference among multiple simultaneously active subordinate swarm-code S1 processes by bounding the active population and feeding observed completion/capacity back into subsequent admission. - Disturbance / variety regulated: a workflow phase may contain more full coding-agent commitments than should execute simultaneously; unbounded spawning competes for CPU, memory, model/provider capacity and other host resources. The implementation explicitly replaced behavior that could fork an entire large phase at once with bounded queued admission. - Distinct S1 units: every launched /flows task is a separate headless swarm-code subprocess running the product&#39;s complete Agent/model/tool loop, with its own prompt/model/process lifecycle. - Inter-S1 disturbance: concurrently running full agent processes consume the same host/provider resource envelope; excessive simultaneous execution creates resource contention/oversubscription even when their semantic tasks are independent. - Attenuating coordination relation: flowsmaxparallel() defines the active cap; launchquota() counts current running and queued tasks and computes free capacity; fillphaseslots() starts only the allowed queued subset; the render loop re-polls status and refills slots after child termination. - Feedback into subsequent S1 behaviour: child process status is refreshed every polling cycle. A running child occupies a slot and prevents further queued S1 admission; once it becomes terminal, the observed free slot permits a later queued S1 to start. The relation therefore changes which S1 is active as a function of current inter-S1 resource occupancy. - Why this is S2-specific rather than generic communication / routing / sequencing / shared state / delegation: the positive claim is not based on having several agents or a workflow queue. It is based on a concrete dynamic attenuation path over simultaneous S1 resource demand: observe active population → compute capacity → admit/withhold another S1 → observe completion → refill. - Decisive decision or feedback right: determine from live task occupancy whether another subordinate S1 may enter the active set now. - Decision owner: deterministic first-party /flows controller. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Background.launch; per-task process/status records; flowsmaxparallel; countrunning; countqueued; launchquota; fillphaseslots; 500 ms status polling. - Closure path: current child-process statuses → running/queued counts → free-slot computation → launch or withhold queued child → changed active population → next poll observes the resulting occupancy. - Boundary reachability: /flows is a documented shipped product command and launches first-party swarm-code binaries; no application-authored coordinator is required beyond supplying workflow data. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: the user/model supplies prompts/workflow content, but the material attenuation decision is mechanically derived from current occupancy and the configured cap. Removing all model judgment does not remove the admission controller, so the function is code-owned. - Evidence: src/Flows.sw; README.md. - Basis: explicit + structural. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: the credited S2 relation is resource/concurrency attenuation. No claim is made that /flows autonomously negotiates semantic conflicts, filesystem overlap or contradictory goals among concurrently running children.\">C</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-c\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: C - Function: regulate the current set and lifecycle of subordinate workflow commitments by maintaining the whole /flows phase/task state, admitting queued commitments into execution, advancing phases only after current commitments terminate and marking the organization complete or aborted. - Disturbance / variety regulated: current workload population, queued versus running commitments, child completion/failure, phase dependencies/order, active-capacity changes and user-requested workflow abort. - Whole-system current view: Flows state contains the complete workflow phase list and every task&#39;s label, prompt/model, child id, current status, exit code, timing and execution statistics. refreshallphases polls the status of all launched tasks across all phases before current-control decisions are made. - Current-control decision scope: the controller launches the first/current phase, selects which queued commitments become active under the current cap, detects when all tasks in the current phase are terminal, starts the next phase, declares the whole flow done after the last phase, and changes the organizational state to aborted on the supported stop signal. - Decisive decision or feedback right: determine which current workflow commitments execute now and when the organization may transition between current operational phases based on observed child state. - Decision owner: deterministic first-party /flows controller operating over an operator/user-authored workflow definition. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: initstate; per-phase/task state; spawnphasetasks; fillphaseslots; refreshallphases; allagentsdone; phase transition logic; stop-file abort; Background process records/logs. - Closure path: all-current task/process state → deterministic controller refreshes and evaluates phase/capacity conditions → launch/withhold/advance/done/abort decision → child/phase state changes → next controller poll observes the changed whole-flow current state. - Boundary reachability: /flows is a standard documented execution surface implemented in the pinned repository and launches the product&#39;s own headless runtime. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: the workflow author supplies objectives/order, but current lifecycle decisions do not require a model to judge whether a phase is complete or which queued task fits a free slot; code owns those decisions from live state. The function is therefore C, not A. - Evidence: src/Flows.sw; src/background.sw; README.md. - Basis: explicit + structural. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: this is a workflow-current-control S3, not an autonomous strategic manager. The workflow topology/prompts are supplied ahead of time, and current decisions are deterministic lifecycle control over that declared commitment structure.\">C</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: — - Function: no material boundary-reachable complementary audit function with both sufficiently independent challenge and a returned corrective-control closure was established. - Disturbance / variety regulated: the experimental council can surface architecture, risk and operational disagreements, but no S3-specific corrective-return loop is closed into production control. - Decisive decision or feedback right: not established for a complementary auditor whose verdict changes current operational commitments. - Decision owner: not established at S3. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: scripts/council.sh; three bounded read-only panel agents; no-tools judge; council-specific ToolRegistry execution contexts; tests/review scripts and ordinary coding/test tools. - Closure path: incomplete. Independent panels → judge synthesis → printed advisory output is established; judge verdict → authoritative corrective change in the primary Agent or /flows controller is not wired in the frozen product. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: the council supplies real independent model judgments, but independence alone is insufficient. A human or some separate caller must interpret/use the final recommendation; swarm-code does not first-party route that result back into a corrective operational decision. - Evidence: scripts/council.sh; src/ToolRegistry.sw; README.md. - Basis: explicit + structural absence review. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: the council is stronger evidence than ordinary unit tests because its panelists are independent model actors with narrowed direct repository access; the negative state is specifically due to missing returned corrective closure, not lack of review independence.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: — - Function: no material first-party external-and-prospective intelligence loop that develops and adopts adaptations to swarm-code&#39;s present organizational capability was established. - Disturbance / variety regulated: scheduling, web access, memory/session search, skills, profiles, MCP and recurring prompts can broaden present execution or defer it in time, but they do not close future-oriented organizational adaptation. - Decisive decision or feedback right: not established for an S4 actor. - Decision owner: not established. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Scheduler; persistent memory; session FTS; skills; web tools; MCP; provider profiles; cron prompts; trajectory export. - Closure path: no environment/future sensing → prospective option generation/evaluation → adopted change to current organizational capability/S3 loop was found. The scheduler merely compares time against operator-authored schedules and fire-and-forgets a predetermined prompt. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: current-task search/memory and scheduled execution reuse configured capabilities. They do not create a separate actor that observes emerging external/future conditions, develops an organizational adaptation and closes its adoption into current control. - Evidence: src/scheduler.sw; README.md; session/memory/skills modules in the pinned tree. - Basis: explicit + structural absence review. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: a user can schedule a prompt such as &quot;review open PRs&quot; and a model may reason prospectively inside that future S1 run; this is not a persistent first-party S4 closure over the product organization itself.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: — - Function: no material first-party runtime identity/ultimate-policy closure was established at the assessed recursion. - Disturbance / variety regulated: trust decisions, permissions, execution contexts, hard command denies, remote-endpoint opt-in, project configuration restrictions and hook/tool policies bound operation but do not constitute runtime identity/ultimate-policy authority. - Decisive decision or feedback right: not established for S5-level identity or ultimate policy. - Decision owner: developer/operator-authored configuration and trust choices remain outside a qualifying runtime S5 actor. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: ToolExecutor; ToolRegistry; trust/untrust configuration; project-config restriction; hardline command blocklist; context allow-lists; permissions/hooks; SWARMCODEALLOWREMOTE and other environment/config flags. - Closure path: absent at S5 level. Externally authored policy → deterministic enforcement is present; runtime identity/policy issue → legitimate ultimate authority → authoritative policy revision → returned organization-wide policy is not. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: models and deterministic controllers operate inside the policy envelope but do not own the legitimate right to redefine swarm-code&#39;s identity or ultimate constitutional rules. Operator trust/permission choices are external governance rather than product-owned S5. - Evidence: src/ToolExecutor.sw; src/ToolRegistry.sw; README.md; SECURITY.md. - Basis: explicit + structural absence review. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: strict fail-closed enforcement is important safety infrastructure but enforcement strength is not equivalent to S5 ownership.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/9a21178777039b6b5176ed68759b59c7f72bd526/assessments/swarm-code.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit 9a21178777039b6b5176ed68759b59c7f72bd526\">9a21178 &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr data-a=\"1\" data-c=\"0\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-11-07T01:58:14+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/shijizhi/picocode\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">PicoCode</a><small>2026</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"1\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>1</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>0</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-11-07\">2026-11-07</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/shijizhi/picocode/tree/c016d4ff9a84c6584f4c09706b05a364b2a5b5dd\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision c016d4ff9a84c6584f4c09706b05a364b2a5b5dd\">c016d4f &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: direct coding investigation, workspace editing and command/test effects in pursuit of a user-specified programming goal. - Disturbance / variety regulated: repository uncertainty, missing symbols/files, build/test failure, changed file contents, insufficient context, provider errors and operator effects policy. - Decisive decision or feedback right: the model selects which real tool and parameters to use next; after results, it can choose to modify files, retry a command, investigate or finalize. - Decision owner: the first-party AgentCore model/tool loop, with semantic action selection delegated to the configured LLM. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: AiClient, TOOLPROTOCOL, native tool schema, ToolRuntime, Workspace, reversible editing, hashed previews, runcommand, event context and limits. - Closure path: user request → LLM tool choice → workspace tool effect/denial → observed result/event → model&#39;s next choice or final response; failed command triggers an explicit same-model repair context. - Boundary reachability: terminal::processsubmission constructs AgentCore in the shipped binary; runpromptturn repeatedly invokes ai.complete and tools.execute. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: without model-directed action selection, the remaining terminal and deterministic tools cannot autonomously decide how to carry out the coding task. - Evidence: wired terminal, core, tools. - Basis: explicit + structural, corroborated by first-party fake-provider loop tests. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: fake-provider unit tests verify control flow, not actual inference-backed task success; Rust toolchain unavailable for execution here.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: — - Function: no independent first-party coordination of interfering operational S1 units is evidenced. - Disturbance / variety regulated: edit checkpoint mismatch, duplicate target in one batch and overlapping human task contexts are local integrity hazards, not demonstrated competition among separately deciding S1 workers. - Distinct S1 units: one AgentCore works per model turn. Session forks are user-selected saved histories, not a wired collaborating cohort. - Inter-S1 disturbance: no shared multi-agent workspace commitment or first-party concurrent autonomous writer collision is specified. - Attenuating coordination relation: expectedhash and atomic batch rollback guard individual file writes; they do not allocate or reconcile independently owned work from multiple S1 agents. - Feedback into subsequent S1 behaviour: the same agent sees its own tool errors, not a coordination verdict balancing different S1 actors. - Why this is S2-specific rather than generic communication / routing / sequencing / shared state / delegation: thread::spawn keeps the UI responsive and edit hashes detect stale reads; neither establishes separate competing decision owners or returned inter-unit arbitration. - Decisive decision or feedback right: no independent first-party cross-S1 interference decision. - Decision owner: none evidenced; hardcoded workspace checks and operator session navigation are S1 support. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: base hashes, checkpoint journal, batch rollback, manual session forks. - Closure path: local edit clash → one tool error → same agent&#39;s next operation, not an inter-agent settlement. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: no multi-worker ownership exists at the frozen operating boundary. - Evidence: workspace conflict checks, terminal dispatch, fork API. - Basis: structural absence review. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: the product may be run in independent terminal processes, but the source does not establish an owned cooperative group of those instances.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: — - Function: no first-party whole-current supervisor allocates resources, priorities and exceptions among a portfolio of S1 operations. - Disturbance / variety regulated: tool/model step limits, command timeout, TUI pending queues, session switching and compaction bound an active turn or human interaction. - Decisive decision or feedback right: no separate current-control actor chooses priorities or reassigns multiple operational commitments; limits and queue processing are fixed. - Decision owner: model for one task, user for session/model choices, source code for static limits. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: AgentConfig, runtime status, event queues, timeouts and session state. - Closure path: agent&#39;s error/time limit returns to its own turn or to user; no whole-current management feedback into distinct operational units. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: a deterministic runtime loop with limits and a TUI progress display does not itself constitute organizational S3. - Evidence: model/tool budgets, TUI dispatch. - Basis: structural absence review. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: interactive human control is outside the assessed first-party model/meta-organization.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: — - Function: no complementary independently grounded evaluator challenges the S1 completion claim and returns binding corrective findings to its controller. - Disturbance / variety regulated: failed shell commands force investigation and retry, while preview/diff/checkpoint guards flag direct edit errors. These are immediate S1 tool observations. - Decisive decision or feedback right: no separate read-only model, deterministic post-hoc cross-checker of completion claims or independent raw-source reviewer is wired. - Decision owner: same acting model for whether a repair succeeded; user may read TUI logs and diff preview. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: repaircontext, runcommand status/stderr, edit preview and session event logs. - Claim being audited: a possible claim that the coding change is done or passes its tests. - Ordinary reporting path: model observes its own command/tool results, updates context and writes a final answer. - Complementary access path: human-visible diff and command output, but no separate first-party contradicting audit actor or report-to-controller route. - Independence boundary: forced repair after a failed command occurs inside the producer&#39;s single model turn, not independently of its operational evidence flow. - Who acts on findings: the original model reacts to the same tool result; human can intervene externally. - Closure path: command failure → error → same-model repair instruction, not separate verifier judgement and corrective veto. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: mandatory repeat after a direct failed action is operational error handling, not independent audit of a prior reported success. - Evidence: repair loop and context, command result reporting. - Basis: structural absence review. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: tests run by the acting model can be useful correctness evidence, but independence has not been established.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: — - Function: no first-party prospective sensing/scenario/option evaluation selects and returns future adaptation of the agent organization. - Disturbance / variety regulated: varying provider/model availability, user-installed skills and evolving codebases influence current task execution or user configuration. - Decisive decision or feedback right: user manually selects model/skills or updates binary; model&#39;s immediate source discovery solves the present task, not the future of the system. - Decision owner: human for software/model/configuration; operating model for task-bound investigation. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: model switcher, skill/capability discovery, session compression, instruction loader and versioning scripts. - Closure path: user loads capability/model or agent reads current code → immediate tool context; no prospective environmental intelligence-to-adaptation verdict is returned. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: loading an existing skill, compaction and choosing providers are not strategic option evaluation with a future-world model. - Evidence: features, capability handling, models. - Basis: structural absence review. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: the design document mentions possible future architecture; aspirational text cannot establish a shipped closed loop.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: — - Function: no distinct first-party ultimate legitimacy/organizational identity policy path resolves mission conflicts and returns ratified direction. - Disturbance / variety regulated: risky commands, workspace escape and model/provider settings are tool-effect limitations, not contested identity. - Decisive decision or feedback right: no ratified mission/policy revision is issued by a legitimate top-level organizational authority inside the harness. - Decision owner: human supplies goal and configuration; deterministic command policy restricts effects; model chooses task operations. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: project CommandApproval, instruction loader, command risk checks and workspace fences. - Closure path: command is allowed/denied by static policy → tool outcome to same S1; no ultimate policy deliberation, ratification or persistent charter update. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: risk gating and user consent per command do not supply an S5 identity-dispute resolution role. - Evidence: config/approval, command gate. - Basis: structural absence review. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: external institutional policies governing a human user are not first-party harness S5.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/b53a912dfd444b654217576c1e1e3e239eec3672/assessments/pi/picocode.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit b53a912dfd444b654217576c1e1e3e239eec3672\">b53a912 &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr data-a=\"1\" data-c=\"0\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-09-26T22:12:29Z\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/CrewBeeLab/Waggle\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Waggle</a><small>2026</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"1\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>1</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>0</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-09-27\">2026-09-27</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/CrewBeeLab/Waggle/tree/db9e3d3aed66714d665fd1c5c40d66534bf44ed7\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision db9e3d3aed66714d665fd1c5c40d66534bf44ed7\">db9e3d3 &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: perform the substantive model-driven task of the governed AgentRun by choosing task-relevant actions/tools from current context, consuming tool observations and continuing until a terminal answer/state. - Disturbance / variety regulated: open-ended user/product task input; model uncertainty; changing workspace/tool results; allowed capability set; model/tool failures; confirmation outcomes; bounded context and cancellation. - Decisive decision or feedback right: choose the next task-specific action/tool call and arguments from the current conversation/observations, then decide whether further actions are required or the run can complete. - Decision owner: the autonomous model actor instantiated through Waggle&#39;s first-party production PiCoreAdapter operating mode. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: external Pi Core loop primitive; Waggle Model Gateway; packed context/system prompt; Waggle tool wrappers; RuntimeToolExecutor; RunPermissionBoundary; Permission Guard; Workspace Guard; event sink; durable run lifecycle and cancellation. - Closure path: AgentRun input/context -&gt; Waggle PiCoreAdapter.execute() constructs model/context/tools -&gt; model chooses a tool/action -&gt; Waggle tool wrapper enters RuntimeToolExecutor -&gt; governed tool result returns as Pi/model observation -&gt; subsequent model turn changes action or produces terminal text -&gt; Waggle completes the run and persists evidence. - Boundary reachability: the README&#39;s documented run/HTTP path starts a real task through the Control Plane, and current-implementation.md identifies local PiCoreAdapter.execute() through Pi Core as current executable production-default runtime behavior rather than target architecture. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Pi Core supplies generic loop mechanics and Waggle supplies policy/lifecycle plumbing, but neither deterministic layer preselects the contextual task action. Removing the model actor while retaining those mechanisms would not yield materially the same tool/argument/termination choices. The external loop library is an implementation dependency, not a separately deployed worker organization whose VSM functions are inherited. - Evidence: README.md; docs/development/current-implementation.md; packages/runtime-pi/src/pi-core-adapter.ts. - Basis: explicit + structural. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: product-specific tools and final business effects remain outside Waggle. The S1 claim covers task decisions within the capabilities exposed to the governed run, not business authority that the README explicitly reserves to the product.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: — - Function: no qualifying inter-S1 coordination function is established in the current generic standard distribution at the assessed AgentRun recursion. - Disturbance / variety regulated: the repository has Project/Session/Run state, queues/workers and future parent-child concepts, but no current first-party witness establishes distinct S1 operational units plus a concrete interaction disturbance and S2-specific attenuation relation. - Decisive decision or feedback right: none established for S2. - Decision owner: none established. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: session/run state; queue/worker records; run orchestration; Tool Proxy; shared evidence; planned parent/child topology. - Closure path: no current distinct-S1 disturbance -&gt; S2-specific coordination judgment -&gt; changed behaviour of the affected S1 units is established. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: ordinary queueing, shared records and run orchestration coordinate software execution, but Methodology 0.3.6 does not award S2/C from generic communication/scheduling primitives. The architecture explicitly places parent-child orchestration in target evolution rather than current executable truth. - Evidence: docs/development/architecture.md; docs/development/current-implementation.md; packages/core/src/runtime/run-orchestrator.ts. - Basis: explicit + structural absence. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: a later implemented parent/child/multi-run organization or downstream product can create a different recursion that requires separate assessment.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: — - Function: no separate first-party whole-system current-control decision loop is established beyond the governed S1 run and deterministic enforcement of preselected constraints. - Disturbance / variety regulated: tool risk, actor/package/workspace mismatch, expired authority, model/tool availability, cancellation, lease/watchdog failures and confirmation-required effects are regulated mechanically or by a local parent decision, but the frozen boundary does not show a manager selecting current priorities/resources/commitments/constraints for the whole from a current operational view. - Decisive decision or feedback right: none established for a qualifying S3 function. - Decision owner: none established for qualifying S3. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: immutable RunPermissionBoundary; Permission Guard; RuntimeToolExecutor; run statuses; worker scheduling/leases; watchdog/recovery; cancellation; user/product confirmations; package/run configuration. - Closure path: configured authority -&gt; deterministic allow/ask/deny and lifecycle enforcement -&gt; current run behavior changes. This is strong enforcement, but no distinct whole-system S3 discretion/feedback loop is supplied. - Boundary reachability: all mechanisms are present in current execution, but they remain pre-authored enforcement/lifecycle paths rather than a separately owned current-control function. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: the model proposes an S1 action; the guard applies already-selected rules; a human/product can approve one specific effect. Removing the putative manager leaves the same deterministic rule enforcement because there is no autonomous manager to remove. Ordinary one-action approval does not satisfy the Methodology&#39;s parent-governed S3 witness. - Whole-system current view: Waggle exposes run/session/events/evidence/attention state, but visibility is not itself a current-control decision right. - Current-control decision scope: cancellation, confirmation and permission effects are local/run-lifecycle decisions; no qualifying resource/priority/accountability/synergy intervention for the whole is established. - Evidence: README.md; packages/core/src/permissions/run-permission-boundary.ts; packages/core/src/permissions/permission-guard.ts; apps/cli/src/run-confirmation-flow.ts; docs/development/current-implementation.md. - Basis: explicit + structural absence. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: product-owned business authority is real, but the evidence at this recursion concerns individual business-effect confirmation/final write or preconfigured rules, not an S3 whole-system current-control function.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: — - Function: no materially independent complementary audit judgment with corrective return into subsequent operation is established in the current runtime. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Waggle records trace, audit, evidence, permission decisions, artifacts and package eval/release state, but these surfaces are produced by or validate the same governed execution/lifecycle paths. - Decisive decision or feedback right: none established for an independent auditor that challenges operational claims from complementary access and returns a correction. - Decision owner: none established. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: trace/audit/evidence stores; governed-run evidence; validation/eval types; package registry validation; confirmation records; hash/provenance checks. - Closure path: operation/control -&gt; records/checks/evidence; no independent audit actor/judgment -&gt; corrective instruction -&gt; changed and rechecked operation loop is supplied. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: logging, deterministic contract validation, package lifecycle checks and human confirmation are not independent operational audit by themselves. - Evidence: docs/development/current-implementation.md; packages/core/src/evals/index.ts; packages/core/src/registry/agent-package-registry-service.ts. - Basis: explicit + structural absence. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: downstream AgentPackages may define reviewers/evals, but application-specific reviewers are not credited to generic Waggle without a first-party standard path that closes independence and corrective feedback.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: — - Function: no first-party prospective environment-facing adaptation loop is established in the current standard operating mode. - Disturbance / variety regulated: model/provider catalogs, package versions, eval declarations, memory and product integrations can change across deployments/runs, but changes are operator/developer-authored or deterministic lifecycle state rather than autonomous sensing of external/future distinctions and option development. - Decisive decision or feedback right: none established for selecting and adopting a future capability/posture based on environmental intelligence. - Decision owner: none established. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: AgentPackage versions; validate/eval/release/rollback/deprecate APIs; model catalog/connections; persistent context/memory; proposals; development hot reload. - Closure path: operator/developer supplies new package/config/version -&gt; Waggle validates/releases/uses it. No environment sensing -&gt; prospective options -&gt; autonomous adaptation judgment -&gt; returned changed present capability loop closes. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: package lifecycle machinery can promote or roll back supplied artifacts, but the frozen runMinimumEval() does not itself generate capability alternatives or adapt from external distinctions. Persistence/reuse and model selection are not S4 by name. - Evidence: packages/core/src/registry/agent-package-registry-service.ts; docs/development/architecture.md; docs/development/current-implementation.md. - Basis: explicit + structural absence. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: AgentPackage lifecycle provides useful construction/release infrastructure, but Methodology C still requires the S4 function itself to be established; a generic package/version API is not enough.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: — - Function: no first-party identity/ultimate-policy decision loop closes at the Waggle-managed run recursion. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Product/AgentPackage policy, allowed/denied tools, user/workspace permissions, model/provider restrictions, confirmation rules and business ownership constrain the run, but ultimate purpose/business identity remains externally authored. - Decisive decision or feedback right: define/revise the ultimate purpose, identity or top-level normative policy of the operating organization itself. - Decision owner: external product/operator/developer; no qualifying first-party S5 closure is established. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: AgentPackage manifest/profile/policy; Project/ProductIntegration records; RunPermissionBoundary; confirmation rules; user identity; product-owned final writes; package release state. - Closure path: product/operator supplies purpose/policy -&gt; Waggle compiles/enforces it. No identity-level dispute/proposal -&gt; authoritative S5 judgment -&gt; returned ultimate policy -&gt; subsequent operation loop closes inside Waggle. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: the README explicitly reserves business rules, user identity, confirmation UX and final writes to the product. This is parent/external authority, but the evidenced runtime return paths are configuration and individual effect authorization, not a genuine identity-level deliberation/decision path required for S5=P. - Evidence: README.md; packages/core/src/permissions/run-permission-boundary.ts; apps/cli/src/run-confirmation-flow.ts. - Basis: explicit + structural absence. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: a downstream product organization may have legitimate S5 authority and use Waggle as enforcement infrastructure. That parent organization is a different system-in-focus; its authority is not converted into generic Waggle S5 merely because Waggle can enforce supplied policy.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/814b7d3c5c0fa49a2d02d4bd47b447e41128c322/assessments/waggle.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit 814b7d3c5c0fa49a2d02d4bd47b447e41128c322\">814b7d3 &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr data-a=\"1\" data-c=\"0\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-09-18T00:51:56+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/madarco/agentbox\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">AgentBox</a><small>2026</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"1\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>1</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>0</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-09-17\">2026-09-17</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/madarco/agentbox/tree/463a6a0a20176147f0b0dbfa8a261aba5e43cbd6\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision 463a6a0a20176147f0b0dbfa8a261aba5e43cbd6\">463a6a0 &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: A. Supported coding agents autonomously perform coding work inside AgentBox-managed boxes. As with other coding-agent control planes in the Index, external model/backend cognition does not turn the assembled harness operation into C when the standard distribution runs autonomous S1 executions.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S2 / coordination: —. The design document explicitly states that preventing boxes from fighting over the same files is manual at the pin. Shared task records, assignment fields, isolated boxes and reconciliation are useful infrastructure, but no implemented S2-specific path is shown that detects/attenuates a concrete inter-S1 collision or dependency disturbance and feeds the result back into subsequent box behavior.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3 / regulation: —. Manager sessions, authoritative task state and lifecycle APIs expose current information/control mechanisms, but the manager&#39;s own brief is explicitly planned and absent. The pinned system therefore does not establish a first-party actor with whole-system current view plus discretionary authority over priorities/resources/commitments.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: —. Timeline and PR observation surfaces do not establish a sufficiently independent first-party audit judgment with corrective closure.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: —. No external-and-prospective organizational intelligence/adaptation loop is implemented at the reviewed boundary.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: —. User objectives, task creation, approval surfaces and external authority are not themselves a runtime identity/ultimate-policy closure loop. The earlier proposal over-promoted outer human authority to P.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/f17df7784f951d191718bb7504b7770132a18a8a/assessments/agentbox.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit f17df7784f951d191718bb7504b7770132a18a8a\">f17df77 &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr data-a=\"1\" data-c=\"0\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-11-02T21:38:24+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/ishan-parihar/operant\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Operant</a><small>2026</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"1\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>1</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>0</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-11-02\">2026-11-02</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/ishan-parihar/operant/tree/fb1777e9b5510448e5680011f8992281af422ff2\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision fb1777e9b5510448e5680011f8992281af422ff2\">fb1777e &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: Convert user/TODO requests into iterative tool, code and subtask actions and revise after observations. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Changing workspace, API/tool failures, deadlines/budgets, failed tests, memory/context gaps and interruption. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Select subsequent tools/code work, invoke a subagent, retry or finish based on observed results. - Decision owner: Operant&#39;s main model actor; optional native child model handles its own bounded delegated work. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Tool registry, ReAct runtime, memory prefetch, tool/result healing, user approvals, event and verification ledger, TODO autonomous driver. - Closure path: User or TODO goal → model chooses action → guarded tool or child execution → feedback and updated task/context state → subsequent model decision. - Boundary reachability: operant chat, operant run --query and operant autonomous are documented first-party CLI modes built on the same core OperantAgent; optional subagents are shipped tool implementations. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Substantive next-step selection belongs to the model, while CLI, tests, deadlines and permissions constrain or schedule its choices. - Evidence: README operating modes, model loop, agent/subagent contracts, autonomous TODO driver. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: No real hosted model call, external channel session or autonomous publishing run was reproduced in this review.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: — - Function: No separately owned inter-S1 interference attenuation loop demonstrated at the native Operant task boundary. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Potential conflicts or duplications between delegated subagent tasks. - Decisive decision or feedback right: No S2-specific cross-agent conflict decision right established. - Decision owner: No qualifying coordination owner distinct from ordinary parent S1 task decomposition. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Bounded child delegation, model-call parallelization, batch tool safety checks and persistent kanban/TODO task views. - Closure path: A child returns work to the ordinary parent model; no separate cross-S1 conflict witness and corrective work-allocation path is established. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: A task list, a subagent spawn or parallel model advice does not itself regulate resource interference between independently viable units. - Evidence: agent and delegation, parallel execution gate, single-agent autonomous driver. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: medium. - Caveats: External Holon role/task scheduling is not owned by the standalone Operant implementation; custom skills might implement coordination but are not credited.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: — - Function: No separately operated whole-system current control function evidenced within the chosen task-agent boundary. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Aggregate workload, acceptance failures, capacity and policy breaches of multiple S1 units. - Decisive decision or feedback right: The autonomous CLI can pause and test one agent&#39;s work; no distinct manager commands a portfolio of S1 units. - Decision owner: No qualifying S3 model/parent owner inside Operant; external Holon is outside the reviewed same-system boundary. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Test-command gate, failure fingerprint, paused status, permission mode, Holon run-lease supervisor adapter and read-only organization views. - Closure path: Failing test changes the next single-agent autonomous tick; Holon supervisor prepare/heartbeat/finish relays an outside kernel&#39;s stage decisions, not an Operant management judgment. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: A self-directed development loop that retries one task is S1 regulation; process/lease management is mechanical execution, with organizational current control delegated to a separate kernel. - Evidence: autonomous control, external supervisor protocol, external read API. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: medium. - Caveats: The external kernel may have S3, but the available public Operant-only evidence cannot be turned into an asserted integrated whole.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: — - Function: No first-party independent task-finding → author correction → re-review closure shown. - Disturbance / variety regulated: False claims that code changes are correct or safely integrated. - Decisive decision or feedback right: No separately owned independent audit verdict over the current author output. - Decision owner: No qualifying S3 runtime reviewer; a background review model curates skills and memory rather than validating the submitted artifact. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Build/test/start verification recipe, output guardrails, unit tests, Mixture-of-Agents advisory opinions and post-turn background skill/memory reviewer. - Closure path: Verification supplies pass/fail to the acting S1 or its autonomous driver; advice and lessons influence a subsequent session without an independent auditor&#39;s findings-to-correction contract. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Test recipes are deterministic and MoA advisers receive the acting transcript, not independent raw execution reality; the background reviewer edits lessons, not the reviewed patch. - Evidence: verification recipe/runner, MoA reference limits, background reviewer, turn-finalizer. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: medium. - Caveats: External Holon verify-stage results are not evidence of an independent first-party Operant reviewer in the selected boundary.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: — - Function: No distinct external-and-prospective organizational intelligence function established. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Future capability mismatch with changing outside environment. - Decisive decision or feedback right: No model-owned prospective option selection and return into system capability at the assessed organizational level. - Decision owner: Background curator/review model updates operational lessons; no S4 strategy decision owner established. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Skill usage/staleness-based curator, LLM skill consolidation, post-turn skill revision, opt-in kernel prompt/subagent lesson refinement and external integration updates. - Closure path: Completed turns can trigger skill/memory or prompt-lesson edits; these retrospective procedural updates lack a separately observed future-environment intelligence/option evaluation loop. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: The background model has local adaptive agency, but learning from completed tool errors or consolidating stale skills is not automatically the VSM outside-and-then intelligence function. - Evidence: background learning, curator, opt-in kernel description. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: medium. - Caveats: This is an explicit boundary distinction, not a denial that the product can learn new skills or change future prompt behavior.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: — - Function: No self-contained ultimate-policy/identity arbitration loop at the standalone Operant boundary. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Disputes about legitimate ultimate purpose and who may revise the governing charter. - Decisive decision or feedback right: No native final charter authority; human configuration sets permissions and external Holon Chair decisions belong to Holon&#39;s kernel. - Decision owner: Ordinary operator for settings; the actual external Holon Chair/kernel, if installed, is not an Operant-owned S5. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Seat policy scopes, native tool approval and refusal, Chair sender verification/command relay, read-only Holon API client. - Closure path: Operator config restricts tools; Chair identity message can be forwarded to external holon plan approve/reject, but the legitimate ultimate-policy decision itself is not made by Operant. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Protected chair channels and per-employee budgets are concrete authorization interfaces, not proof of an Operant identity constitution or model-owned S5 decision process. - Evidence: Chair channel adapter, Holon client boundary, org mode boundary README. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: high for exclusion of borrowed ownership, medium for native absence. - Caveats: A separate assessment of an independently sourced, explicitly integrated Holon+Operant deployment could establish a parent-assisted S5 arrangement; that is not this source boundary.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/f503f859bc55a809db82b51a21da816f2b056f59/assessments/op/operant.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit f503f859bc55a809db82b51a21da816f2b056f59\">f503f85 &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr data-a=\"1\" data-c=\"1\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-10-23T17:35:01+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/yottadynamics/yottacode\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">yottacode</a><small>2026</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"1\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>1</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>1</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-10-23\">2026-10-23</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/yottadynamics/yottacode/tree/11ffd5884a2827636d54b14c0d60d53c9c93c5d8\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision 11ffd5884a2827636d54b14c0d60d53c9c93c5d8\">11ffd58 &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: select and execute concrete code-repository actions in response to an operator task and changing tool observations. - Disturbance / variety regulated: unknown source layout, errors, evolving diffs, model/provider feedback, nested subtasks and conflicting tool outcomes. - Decisive decision or feedback right: the model in each main or child loop selects follow-up inspections, edits, tool actions and final replies. - Decision owner: active first-party main or child model-backed agent. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: registry of tools, conversation loop, AgentTool, role prompts, permission modes, transcript retention, worktrees, provider adapters and sandbox options. - Closure path: request → model-selected tool → tool execution/outcome → model&#39;s next decision or final result; child results may feed back to the parent model. - Boundary reachability: shipped interactive and one-shot entry points construct and run the first-party agent loop; subagent roles use the real AgentTool child runner, not example-only code. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: the model decides operational actions based on changing feedback while Go tool handlers constrain and implement effects. - Evidence: loop.go, agenttool.go, prompt.go. - Basis: frozen first-party implementation and deterministic source tests; no live LLM-agent runs. - Confidence: high on reachable mechanism; no task-success estimate. - Caveats: results depend on a configured external model and permissions; scripted tests do not show effectiveness in deployment.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-c\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: C - Function: attenuate concurrent write interference among separately deciding child agents in opt-in dispatch. - Disturbance / variety regulated: two or more independently active write workers might edit the same project file, racing or invalidating integration. - Distinct S1 units: independent model-backed dispatch write workers, each with its own child loop, prompt, tool results, worktree and branch. - Inter-S1 disturbance: overlapping edit ownership across task partitions or even across independently started dispatch batches could produce collisions. - Attenuating coordination relation: each worker is assigned a declared file set; source validation rejects overlaps before spawning and checks claims against already running tasks, while write filters confine changed paths and worktrees isolate git state. - Feedback into subsequent S1 behaviour: an invalid colliding batch is not admitted; admitted children work inside disjoint scopes, and parent receives committed branch outcomes or explicit error/conflict results to adjust its subsequent work. - Why this is S2-specific rather than generic communication / routing / sequencing / shared state / delegation: the partition validation actively suppresses the concrete inter-agent disturbance of conflicting file ownership rather than merely routing a message or displaying progress. - Decisive decision or feedback right: reject a conflicting write scope and enforce each admitted worker&#39;s allowed writes; the model chooses proposed partitions but cannot override the gate. - Decision owner: deterministic first-party Go runtime C, not an autonomous coordination agent. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: validateWritePartition, registry running-claim inspection, worker-specific write options, isolated worktrees, commit-status reporting and conflict-stop integration. - Closure path: model-proposed multiworker task → validate ownership / running claims → accept disjoint agents or reject overlap → isolated worker outputs → parent-selected integration and conflict feedback. - Boundary reachability: supported --experimental dispatch registers DispatchTool and integrate; actual AgentTool children, task registry and worktree machinery are called by the tool implementation. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: fixed first-party checks enforce S1 coexistence; agent initiative in proposing a split is not ownership of the inter-unit collision rule. - Evidence: dispatchtool.go, tasks.go, integratetool.go, dispatchtooltest.go. - Basis: frozen code and deterministic Go tests, subject to actual test-run status recorded in the review receipt. - Confidence: medium-high for declared-file enforcement; low for universal merge safety. - Caveats: experimental opt-in; partition depends on supplied file declarations and cannot guarantee semantic compatibility across disjoint files. Ordinary Agent fan-out is not protected by this dispatch guard; conflicts can still stop integrate.\">C</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-p\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: P - Function: monitor and intervene in the currently running agent cohort as a whole at one attended TUI session boundary. - Disturbance / variety regulated: runaway, stuck, costly or incorrectly prioritized parallel children could continue consuming time/resources or modifying work after the operator sees they should stop. - Decisive decision or feedback right: the human views every current task in the live /subagents roster and selectively cancels an individual running child or all workers in a named dispatch batch. - Decision owner: attended TUI human operator P. The model can create work partitions and integrate results, but lacks a demonstrated model-owned selective current-cohort intervention function. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: shared subagents.Registry with status/activity/batch identity/cancel hooks, TUI dock/picker and /subagents stop command, Cancel/CancelBatch using child context cancellation. - Closure path: task registry captures current workers → operator examines live progress → /subagents stop &lt;id&gt; or stop batch &lt;id&gt; → child cancellation request → affected running child stops on context check while unrelated active tasks continue. - Boundary reachability: attended TUI registers cmdSubagents and the picker backed by the same session task registry used by foreground/background AgentTool and DispatchTool. - Whole-system current view: the local registry lists all currently active session child agents including independent dispatch batches, statuses, last activity, branch and resource usage, rather than only one child&#39;s report. - Current-control decision scope: an operator can revoke an ongoing child commitment individually, or a whole named batch, without canceling all unrelated active tasks; control is local-session scoped and cancellation is cooperative. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: the intervention is selected by the human from live current-state observations; the registry and cancel API enforce but do not autonomously choose the management action. - Evidence: cmdsubagents.go, tasks.go, subagentdock.go, subagentspicker.go. - Basis: source-verified operator control and deterministic registry/command tests; not a human-in-the-loop execution trial. - Confidence: medium-high for attended local-session control; no claim of autonomous S3 or distributed orchestration. - Caveats: TUI dependent; cancellation is cooperative and worker commit finalization can be cancellation-detached briefly, so the request is not a synchronous kill guarantee. One-shot lacks this live attended roster.\">P</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: — - Function: no verified sufficiently independent audit of operational claims with a corrective return to current-control authority. - Disturbance / variety regulated: a worker could claim a patch passes checks while its implementation is wrong or incomplete; the on-demand code-review tool may identify some such faults. - Decisive decision or feedback right: /code-review asks a producing session&#39;s model to launch fresh review/verifier subagents and synthesize findings; no first-party independent S3 audit mandate or control over the audit scope and disposition was shown. - Decision owner: no distinct S3 owner established; local reviewer child agents may exercise ordinary S1 verification choices inside the originating production workflow. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: codereviewcontext, separate read-only reviewer contexts, prompt-driven adversarial code-verifiers, diff snapshot construction and final textual report. - Closure path: user/requested local diff → model-chosen review fan-out → findings returned to the same originating session; no independently controlled complementary reality path coupled to a responsible S3 decision is established. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: model-backed review is real, but a separately prompted critic does not automatically become a complementary organizational audit independent of the operation it reviews. - Evidence: codereviewworkflow.go, codereview.go, cmdcodereview.go. - Basis: frozen first-party wiring and prompt design. - Confidence: medium, bounded to session-level ownership, not external review governance. - Caveats: independent reviewer contexts and raw source access are valuable but do not prove independent audit initiation/closure; third-party review or a deliberately separate operating session is outside this boundary.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: — - Function: no first-party strategic external/future sensing that develops adaptation options and returns them to current capability decisions. - Disturbance / variety regulated: shifts in future demand, external providers, competition, tools or research are not a demonstrated ongoing adaptation process at this local harness recursion. - Decisive decision or feedback right: the model may research, plan or consult tools for the present user task, while maintainers or users choose any longer-run product redesign. - Decision owner: none established for local-session S4. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: web/MCP tools, memory, plan/explore agents, repository search, advisor-model routing and prompt macros. - Closure path: observations about external materials serve a current coding task; no persistent external/future distinction → adaptation proposal → S3 capability negotiation/update channel is wired. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: research or planning by a current task actor does not entail future-facing organizational intelligence and operating-capability evolution. - Evidence: agenttool.go, prompt.go, architecture.md. - Basis: bounded first-party source/design review. - Confidence: medium. - Caveats: user can ask a model to do strategy research; this is not evidence of a shipped self-directed S4 capability-update loop.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: — - Function: no first-party ultimate organizational identity decision with legitimate closure over this assessed operating session. - Disturbance / variety regulated: permissions and unsafe commands need tactical gating, but that is not the system-identity and constitutional choice relevant to S5. - Decisive decision or feedback right: users choose permission modes, allow specific tool actions, change provider config and ask for or stop tasks; upstream maintainers set software defaults. None is shown making a legitimate identity-level decision fed into S3 and S4. - Decision owner: no in-boundary ultimate-policy or identity authority established. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: auto/plan/yolo permission modes, approval prompts, dispatch unattended policy, command hardline floor, optional container sandbox. - Closure path: action request → permission check → allow or deny a tool execution; no durable S5 identity deliberation → cross-functional policy closure path is evidenced. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: hardcoded safety defaults and operator consent for individual executions do not confer organization-wide identity decision rights on the acting model. - Evidence: dispatch.md, tui-slash-commands.md, agenttool.go. - Basis: bounded source/review of approvals and distribution modes. - Confidence: medium-high for rejecting tool permission as S5. - Caveats: independent enterprise governance may be layered over the tool but cannot be credited to this first-party session.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/daa280a2f14b5e683b058defd70ad145aa7492f0/assessments/yo/yottacode.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit daa280a2f14b5e683b058defd70ad145aa7492f0\">daa280a &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr data-a=\"1\" data-c=\"0\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-10-20T17:38:43+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/formiat/multi-agent-orchestrator-rs\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">multi-agent-orchestrator-rs</a><small>2026</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"1\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>1</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>0</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-10-20\">2026-10-20</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/formiat/multi-agent-orchestrator-rs/tree/ea6c114a40c005256d125984b3d77cc758bdc8fe\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision ea6c114a40c005256d125984b3d77cc758bdc8fe\">ea6c114 &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: Per-request planning, investigation or implementation of software tasks, producing investigation/plan documents or local code changes with feedback-directed repair. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Variable repository behavior, complex task requirements, defects, missing artifacts, regressions, rejected reviewer checks and uncertain provider session continuity. - Decisive decision or feedback right: A configured external-provider executor agent reasons about the specific task, inspects the workspace and edits/commits or writes the required artifact; a separately bound reviewer agent independently examines actual changes, chooses an evidence-backed YAML accept/revise/blocked/disagreement decision and sends concrete improvement feedback. The first-party Rust gate only interprets structured decisions and deterministic conditions, and retries the executor on revise. - Decision owner: The task-dependent choices and corrections are owned by the two configured AI agent sessions (Claude/OpenCode/Codex) under first-party prompt templates and provider dispatch in this distributed execution mode. The Rust orchestrator is the deterministic harness controller; the human supplies the prompt, target repo, model/provider options, session identities and network policies. These external provider tools are indispensable executors, not a first-party in-process model implementation. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Source-owned workflow templates, CLI input and session lock/binding, transport files and fingerprints, provider subprocess adapters, FSM monitor, reviewer YAML schema and quality gate, Git checks, retry cap, batch task-file queue. - Closure path: CLI workspace/prompt and preexisting sessions → first-party context preparation + executor prompt → provider-session executor makes and reports task changes → first-party monitor/transport → independent reviewer-session checks code/files/tests → parsed review decision → Rust quality gate closes success or dispatches feedback into a later executor round. Batch mode repeats per queued task, not autonomously planning a new organization. - Boundary reachability: Main binary invokes first-party orchestrator; current orchestrator phases dispatch the two supported provider sessions with owned templates and transport; accept/revise flow in quality gate terminates or re-enters executor dispatch. Batch binary invokes the same orchestration workflow for each task file. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Removing the configured agent sessions eliminates the discretionary investigation/coding and semantic review; Rust cannot invent equivalent task-specific plans, code or critique. Conversely removing the Rust FSM leaves uncoordinated provider sessions without shipped restart/review-contract/gate behavior. Agent discretion is first-party integrated at the supported runtime boundary, not Rust inference. - Evidence: README.md; src/main.rs; src/orchestrator/mod.rs; src/workflow/executor.rs; src/workflow/reviewer.rs; src/orchestrator/phases/dispatch.rs; src/orchestrator/phases/output.rs; src/orchestrator/phases/decision.rs; crates/orchestrate-batch/src/lib.rs; src/providers/mod.rs; src/yamlcheck.rs - Basis: explicit and structural — executable prompt/dispatch/review/feedback chain - Confidence: High for source-wired agentic S1; provider session runtime effects not live-tested. - Caveats: Requires two separate existing authenticated provider sessions, CLI binaries and an appropriate clean Git worktree. No provider inference or real target modifications were performed; the repo&#39;s documented behavior and first-party integration code, not measured output quality, ground this result.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: — - Function: No sustained coordination function shown that attenuates actual inter-S1 oscillation or mutual disturbance among multiple independently viable operational units. - Disturbance / variety regulated: An executor may miss a requirement and reviewer may request revisions; batches contain several sequential software tasks. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Reviewer feedback governs acceptance/revision for the same coding task, not mutual operating interference between separate viable S1 departments. Sequential batch runner selects the next numbered task after completion but does not maintain interdependent S1 mutual constraints. - Decision owner: Reviewer session owns a task-local judgment; Rust quality gate routes retry and the queue determines sequence. No distinct S2 variety-attenuating owner between viable units. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Role separation and transport protocol, review YAML accept/revise contract, fixed FSM, sequential batch task processing, per-session locks. - Closure path: Executor work → reviewer feedback → same executor&#39;s corrective next round → terminal result; not S1-A disturbance → S2 negotiation/coordination → modified concurrent S1-B and S1-A behavior. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Executor and reviewer are asymmetric production and quality-control roles in one work cell. Review is not coordination of two ongoing independently viable systems. Multiple queue entries are tasks rather than independently operating environmental departments. - Evidence: src/workflow/reviewer.rs; src/workflow/executor.rs; src/orchestrator/phases/decision.rs; crates/orchestrate-batch/src/lib.rs; crates/orchestrate-batch/src/queue.rs; src/orchestrator/mod.rs - Basis: bounded negative with positive task-coordination hypothesis checked - Confidence: High at bounded coding-workflow recursion. - Caveats: Teams running several instances could externally coordinate across products, but no shipped cross-instance organizational S2/feedback loop is assessed.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: — - Function: No separately justified whole-system present management of continuing viable operational units and binding cross-unit resources/commitments. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Provider hangs, malformed YAML, dirty worktree, regressions, policy violations and failed task deliveries. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Rust decides state transitions/retries, scoring thresholds, termination, no-progress caps, and error reporting from a fixed configuration. Reviewer decides if one task is acceptable. Human selects CLI constraints before running; no actor evaluates and directs whole-current multi-unit production/resource/accountability trade-offs. - Decision owner: Task reviewer agent for bounded product gate, deterministic Rust FSM for mechanical retry/failure and user for input policies. None is shown exercising the organizational S3 management right. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: FSM run states, monitoring/probe diagnostics, reviewer schema/quality score, session locks, workspace clean preflight, provider options and network policy. - Closure path: One task/reviewer output → deterministic gate/retry/failure → same task executor or terminal JSON; no plural operational-unit management returns with allocations or policy reconciliations. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: A quality gate over one deliverable is not whole-current S3 merely because it blocks acceptance. Network policies and retry thresholds originate outside the agent, and code enforces rather than autonomously decides organization-wide management. - Evidence: src/orchestrator/mod.rs; src/orchestrator/phases/decision.rs; src/fsm.rs; src/state.rs; src/orchestrator/phases/preflight.rs; src/cli.rs; src/orchestrator/monitor.rs; crates/orchestrate-batch/src/lib.rs - Basis: bounded negative after model, deterministic and human paths - Confidence: High. - Caveats: An operator could use the tool inside a multi-team organization, but that parent&#39;s present resource-management authority is not wired as an assessed first-party mode.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: — - Function: No distinct complementary investigation of an S3 management view or its executive claims against unfiltered operational reality with corrective return. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Executor self-report may misstate implementation quality, omit changed files, hide regressions or violate policies. - Decisive decision or feedback right: The separately bound reviewer is explicitly required to inspect workspace artifacts, code, diffs and risks independently and issue revise/accept/block decisions. This is strong task-level quality verification, but it samples executor output, not claims in a distinct organizational S3 present-management account. - Decision owner: Independent reviewer agent owns a real S1 quality-review decision. The deterministic gate applies it to the same work item; no S3 mandate/recipient at the current whole-system management recursion. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Reviewer prompts with no-trust requirement, workspace/commit checks, YAML structured findings, review repair and procedural error gates. - Closure path: Executor artifact and claims → independent reviewer evidence → YAML judgment → executor correction / terminal; not S3 management self-report → independently sampled unit reality → corrective report into continuing S3 controls. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Do not discard a real independent reviewer: its audit-like behavior is positive evidence for robust S1 control. Its measured object, decision scope, and corrective recipient remain task delivery, not S3 managerial communications or an independent S3 audit function. - Evidence: src/workflow/reviewer.rs; src/workflow/executor.rs; src/yamlcheck.rs; src/orchestrator/phases/output.rs; src/orchestrator/phases/decision.rs; src/orchestrator/mod.rs - Basis: explicit source evidence of task verification, negative S3 function adjudication - Confidence: High with fixed recursion and current management assessment. - Caveats: One could change the boundary or introduce a manager whose reports are audited; no such parent pathway exists as shipped code.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: — - Function: No continuing future-environment intelligence with accepted strategic change to the organization’s capability or policies. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Unknown technical constraints may emerge in investigations; provider models may vary, external Git/Notion/remote systems may change. - Decisive decision or feedback right: An executor can research and create an investigation or implementation plan for the current user-supplied task. Model override, provider selection, network policy and prompt/workflow type are operator inputs; neither S4 agent nor parent accepts a strategy governing future work of the whole system. - Decision owner: Configured executor model for immediate task analysis and user/developer for CLI/provider policy; no standing outward/future strategy owner. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Investigate and plan workflow types, provider CLI adapters, project profiles, Notion/remote access protocols, task wrappers and current workspace discovery. - Closure path: Current request → case investigation/PLAN.md → reviewer quality feedback → file artifact/implementation. No outside trends → future strategic options → legitimate acceptance → changed future ongoing organizational operating capability. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: The words &#39;plan&#39;, &#39;investigate&#39;, and &#39;research&#39; can denote future thinking about a software change but refer to scoped S1 user work, not the viable organization&#39;s environmental strategy and accepted renewal. - Evidence: src/workflow/executor.rs; src/workflow/reviewer.rs; src/cli.rs; crates/orchestrate-batch/src/lib.rs; src/orchestrator/phases/preflight.rs; README.md - Basis: bounded negative with task research positive hypothesis evaluated - Confidence: High. - Caveats: Outputs may inform human strategic decisions externally; no first-party S4 acceptance/return is provided.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: — - Function: No ultimate organizational identity/purpose-policy determination or legitimate parent adjudication/return loop. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Conflicting user requirements, provider selection, remote network safety, quality thresholds, blocked/irreconcilable reviewer outcomes. - Decisive decision or feedback right: External operator sets workflow, providers, sessions and remote network policy. Static Rust/embedded prompts enforce no-forbidden remote actions, reviewer acceptance rules, hard-block stopping and manual next-action reporting; no actor decides the organization&#39;s enduring constitutional purposes and returns revised identity policy into execution. - Decision owner: External human/developer for configuration/policy input; deterministic runtime enforces it and reviewer rejects violations. No first-party S5 agent or legitimate parent identity-policy role. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: CLI flags, remote-network policy enum, baked-in reviewer instructions and quality thresholds, session locks, safety gates and terminal error reporting. - Closure path: Operator-provided constraints → task-level enforcement/stop or reviewer verdict → terminal report for operator; no ultimate identity issue → valid top authority resolves enduring purpose → adopted constitutional policy changes subsequent system operation. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: A strong deterministic permission check, a quality standard, a disagreement outcome and an external operator do not by themselves form S5; they lack the ultimate-policy object/authority/closure. - Evidence: src/cli.rs; src/workflow/reviewer.rs; src/workflow/executor.rs; src/orchestrator/phases/decision.rs; src/orchestrator/mod.rs; README.md - Basis: bounded negative after parent-mode investigation - Confidence: High. - Caveats: The user can govern their own external organization, but it is not a first-party parent-governed constitutional mode of this released orchestrator.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/7479e63272154f37cb01a8fb89874edce89067b1/assessments/mu/multi-agent-orchestrator-rs.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit 7479e63272154f37cb01a8fb89874edce89067b1\">7479e63 &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr data-a=\"1\" data-c=\"0\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-10-20T01:39:18+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/cubeplexai/cubeloop\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">CubeLoop</a><small>2026</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"1\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>1</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>0</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-10-20\">2026-10-20</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/cubeplexai/cubeloop/tree/eb798f9a20c1f516d6a59a991a63a9d4c630f4bf\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision eb798f9a20c1f516d6a59a991a63a9d4c630f4bf\">eb798f9 &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: Goal-facing model-selected task operations through first-party bound model/tool feedback, with optional ephemeral delegated subagents and current-goal evaluator continuation. - Disturbance / variety regulated: New user requests, changing tool execution results, exceptions, model fallback, context window pressure, streaming interruptions, human tool admission, pending task state and checkpoints. - Decisive decision or feedback right: The configured bound model chooses which registered tool call to issue or when to finish; child-bound-model runs choose task-local actions; the optional goal evaluator returns an achieved/unmet verdict and injects current-task feedback. - Decision owner: Model in a first-party Agent loop has per-goal tool-choice discretion; host configures tools, middleware, provider, model and approval. The ephemeral child model is an S1 task extension, not a separate metasystem. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: runagentloop / runloopinner model streaming, tool-cycle and tool result append; Agent.prompt session, middleware callbacks, concurrently executable tool calls, checkpoint/resume and provider fallback; GoalMiddleware onrunend verification and child delegation. - Closure path: User message → BoundModel stream/generate → assistant tool choices → first-party tool execution/exception reporting → observed result becomes subsequent context → model next choice or final answer; child delegation returns an ephemeral child output. Optional goal evaluator can feed a not-yet-achieved reason back and restart current-goal work. - Boundary reachability: Published Agent(model=BoundModel, tools=[...], middleware=[...]) and prompt() call the runtime; actual Anthropic/OpenAI adapters exist. Child model execution, GoalMiddleware and provider failover are optional but wired first-party. This is not inferred from docs or test fixtures. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Without configured model discretion the remaining session/checkpoint/approval machinery could not select novel goal-sensitive tool paths. A real model must be configured, but the first-party harness provides reachable running infrastructure. - Evidence: cubeloop/agent/agent.py; cubeloop/agent/loop.py; cubeloop/agent/tools.py; cubeloop/providers/base.py; cubeloop/providers/openai.py; cubeloop/providers/anthropic.py; cubeloop/middleware/subagents.py; cubeloop/middleware/goal.py; cubeloop/session/session.py. - Basis: Explicit structural runtime wiring, corroborated by runnable examples; no task-quality experiment run. - Confidence: High for S1 model/feedback architecture. - Caveats: Model provider availability, model correctness and external app tool semantics are not reproduced. Ephemeral subagent capability does not automatically establish a stable second viable organizational S1 department.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: — - Function: No independently governed inter-operating-unit coordination that dampens demonstrated conflicts among distinct viable S1 departments at this framework recursion. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Concurrent tools/child requests, inconsistent results and user steering can disrupt one task, but distributed multi-department interference is not established. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Agent tool concurrency and deferred dispatch decide task execution order; child subagents return findings to parent. No S2-specific decision regarding shared commitments among separately viable departmental S1s. - Decision owner: Caller-configured parent model and runtime sequencing/dispatch; no separately identified S2 coordinating owner across viable S1s. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Parallel/sequential tool execution, SubagentMiddleware, shared tools, deferred tool groups, todo guard and execution-session queue. - Closure path: Tool and child outcomes return to the same parent task; session consumer ordering and tool batching do not close an inter-S1 constraint/oscillation feedback loop. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Subagent instances are ephemeral task-service workers with model and tools, not independently continuing viable organizational departments under a common reciprocal operating regime. Task-level scheduling is not S2. - Evidence: cubeloop/middleware/subagents.py; cubeloop/agent/loop.py; cubeloop/agent/tools.py; cubeloop/deferred/middleware.py; cubeloop/middleware/todo.py; cubeloop/session/session.py. - Basis: Bounded negative at the shipped SDK Agent/task boundary. - Confidence: High. - Caveats: An external multi-agent product might use CubeLoop as its substrate and supply real inter-unit coordination; that external arrangement is not within this source-bound assessment.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: — - Function: No first-party current whole-system operational-management loop owning a portfolio of independently viable S1s, resource choices and returned departmental obligations. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Run failures, tool-call admission, concurrent execution, user controls, agent steering/follow-ups and task checkpoint errors threaten present task completion. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Agent chooses goal-local actions, caller/HITL middleware may approve or deny one tool, user can steer/abort/resume. ExecutionSession arbitrates one agent&#39;s attempts, not discretionary current-resource allocation across S1 departments. - Decision owner: External host/user selects tool exposure, policy, execution mode and the model; middleware supplies admission guard; no distinct whole-current S3 organizational owner. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: ApprovalPolicyMiddleware, ConfirmToolCallMiddleware, session single-attempt lock, persisted input receipts, middleware todo finalization guards, checkpoint/recovery. - Closure path: Operation request → approval/denial or interruption → same task accepts/block/resume; no aggregate viable-S1 status → S3 authoritative allocation → changed operating units and feedback. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Interactive HITL decisions and deterministic session arbitration are concrete but functionally local. A configurable human approval channel is not by itself an S3=P parent-managed organization. - Evidence: cubeloop/hitl/middleware.py; cubeloop/hitl/channel.py; cubeloop/session/session.py; cubeloop/agent/agent.py; cubeloop/middleware/todo.py; cubeloop/checkpointer/base.py. - Basis: Bounded absence after checking supported HITL, session and delegated child modes. - Confidence: High. - Caveats: An embedding application can place an external manager above multiple CubeLoop agents; its decisions cannot be counted as this SDK&#39;s native S3.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: — - Function: No complementary independently owned direct-operating-reality audit with correction returned to a distinct S3 owner. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Current runs can produce misleading completion statements or obscure tool faults and paused approvals. - Decisive decision or feedback right: GoalMiddleware optionally asks a separate evaluator model whether a single task goal is met from a recent transcript; it may resume that very task. Tracing records spans and HITL history; neither supplies independent system-operating reality discovery to current S3 management. - Decision owner: Optional evaluator BoundModel is owned by the goal middleware and governs only completion of a given requested goal, not an S3 audit mandate. Tracing is host-attached instrumentation. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: GoalMiddleware verdict, tracing/Recorder/Tracer, tool event streams, todo finalization guard and session receipts. - Closure path: Transcript → goal judge → goal-current-task continuation/stop. Trace → observability sink. There is no independently checked operational reality → adjudicated management correction → S3 decision returned to operating units. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Even though a second model evaluates task outcome, it sees the supplied conversation, not direct organizational reality under independent audit authority. Mere judge separation does not establish S3; there is no first-party S3 manager receiving an audit challenge. - Evidence: cubeloop/middleware/goal.py; cubeloop/tracing/tracer.py; cubeloop/middleware/todo.py; cubeloop/session/session.py; cubeloop/hitl/middleware.py. - Basis: Bounded negative with explicit inspection of the plausible evaluator/audit path. - Confidence: Medium-high. - Caveats: If deployed with externally mandated trace reviewers and management correction, a bigger composite boundary may have S3; it is not shipped by the bounded SDK.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: — - Function: No shipped owner and closure for future-facing external intelligence and accepted reconfiguration of subsequent organizational capability. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Provider failures, task complexity, rising context costs, new task types or changing operational surroundings would motivate later platform adaptation. - Decisive decision or feedback right: BoundModel fallback picks an alternate configured provider on failure; child agents handle current delegated work; goal evaluator continues current task, todo updates current task steps. None adopts a new future strategy/method for subsequent operating runs. - Decision owner: Host developer supplies fallback chain, middleware and tools in advance; no first-party prospective adaptive strategy process makes and returns a decision. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: FallbackBoundModel, goal evaluation feedback, task todo plan, optional compaction, cached state and middleware hook plumbing. - Closure path: Failure→configured fallback inside same request, goal judge→same request continuation; no boundary-spanning outside surveillance → future option → legitimate acceptance → changed next-run operating rules. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Runtime retrying/fallback or agent revising a TODO list within one goal is S1 adaptation, not VSM outside-and-then S4. The dev/spec/plan files document future engineering intent, not shipped executable S4 ownership. - Evidence: cubeloop/providers/fallback.py; cubeloop/middleware/goal.py; cubeloop/middleware/todo.py; cubeloop/agent/agent.py; cubeloop/middleware/base.py; README.md. - Basis: Bounded negative after inspecting shipped candidate future-strategy routes. - Confidence: High for current packaged modes. - Caveats: Cross-run learning introduced by downstream host code is not evidenced by the pinned first-party framework; a future release may change this.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: — - Function: No first-party legitimate identity/ultimate-purpose policy-authoring owner with authoritative return into the framework&#39;s subsequent organizational operation. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Operators must decide which tools and models to expose, when to require human approval and how to handle agents&#39; data. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Tool approvals can approve/deny/edit one call; application integrator configures system prompt, permissions and middleware. No user/parent procedure for binding constitutional policy or identity changes across viable S1s is implemented. - Decision owner: User/integrator owns purpose and application governance outside this SDK; runtime&#39;s policy callbacks only execute configured local task admission. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Agent(systemprompt=...,model=...,tools=...), HITL ApprovalDecision (Approve/Deny/AskUser), checkpointer, tool registry and examples. - Closure path: User/developer config → bounded agent operation; no ultimate mission/policy question → legitimate S5 decision → identity/charter enforcement returned to subsequent organizational actors. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: A human editing a tool request, setting task goals or changing a config knob is not a first-party S5 parent-governed mode. There is no preserved global decision mandate, identity-level dispute route or returned policy for an autonomous organization. - Evidence: cubeloop/agent/agent.py; cubeloop/hitl/middleware.py; cubeloop/hitl/channel.py; cubeloop/session/session.py; README.md. - Basis: Bounded absence across published host-configuration and HITL modes. - Confidence: High. - Caveats: Organizational governance of CubePlex product customers can provide S5 outside this Index review boundary; cannot borrow it into CubeLoop.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/661548addf330e3e588b06e5d8f19b68d1dfff42/assessments/cu/cubeloop.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit 661548addf330e3e588b06e5d8f19b68d1dfff42\">661548a &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr data-a=\"1\" data-c=\"1\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-09-28T23:42:27Z\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/sausheong/harness\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Harness</a><small>2026</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"1\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>1</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>1</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-09-29\">2026-09-29</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/sausheong/harness/tree/1e5b6599bbc68519e83d156150d70f793653ca7d\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision 1e5b6599bbc68519e83d156150d70f793653ca7d\">1e5b659 &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: perform open-ended agent work through a model-driven decision/action/observation loop with first-party tool execution and continuation. - Disturbance / variety regulated: user/host goals, evolving session state, tool/service/file observations, model uncertainty, tool errors, provider responses, context pressure and runtime budget constraints. - Decisive decision or feedback right: choose the next substantive answer or tool action in light of current context and returned tool observations. - Decision owner: the autonomous model-driven agent actor running inside Harness&#39;s first-party Runtime. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Runtime.Run / RunTurn, provider abstraction, tool registry/executor, permission checker, session persistence, compaction, budgets, result spilling, fallback model and lifecycle hooks. - Closure path: host/user goal → model turn → model-selected tool call or answer → Harness validates/executes the allowed tool → tool result is appended to session → later model turn sees the observation and revises/continues → terminal answer or bounded stop. - Boundary reachability: Runtime.Run and RunTurn are the core shipped library API and require no repository-development actor; a host only supplies the external provider/tools/session expected by the library contract. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: deterministic runtime code transports, bounds and executes actions, but it does not choose the substantive next task action. Removing the model actor leaves execution machinery without the open-ended operational decision loop. - Evidence: runtime/runturn.go; runtime/runtime.go; runtime/builder.go. - Basis: explicit + structural. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: the model/provider itself is environment. S1 credit rests on Harness owning the first-party action/execution/observation continuation path around that model.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: — - Function: no first-party coordination function was established that attenuates a specific interference/oscillation between distinct S1 units at the declared recursion. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Harness can create parent and child Runtime units, but the reviewed standard distribution does not identify a concrete inter-unit conflict and a function-specific coordination judgment/feedback path for resolving it. - Decisive decision or feedback right: not established for S2. - Decision owner: not established. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: task delegation, separate child sessions, recursion depth caps, tool-call concurrency classification, per-Runtime runMu, persistent-session writer leases and shared event forwarding. - Closure path: delegation returns child output to the parent, and locks/serialization protect runtime data structures, but no qualifying inter-S1 disturbance → coordination response → changed later S1 behavior loop was established. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: delegation and transport are not S2 by themselves. The strongest collision-like mechanisms are deterministic safety primitives: the task tool is marked non-concurrency-safe because parallel child-event forwarding is untested, while persistent writer leases protect one session from concurrent writers rather than coordinating the distinct parent/child S1 units that normally use separate sessions. - Evidence: tool/task.go; runtime/subagent.go; session/lease.go. - Basis: explicit + structural. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: a caller application could construct multi-agent collision regulation around these primitives; generic programmability is not enough for C.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: — - Function: no first-party whole-system current-control organ was established at the declared application recursion. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Harness bounds individual Runtime work through turn/token/time/tool concurrency, permissions, steering and cancellation, but these do not form a whole-system view and decision loop over multiple current commitments/resources. - Decisive decision or feedback right: choose or revise current organization-wide allocations, commitments, priorities, constraints or interventions from a whole-system view. - Decision owner: not established. Host/application code chooses configuration and may supply steering; deterministic Harness code enforces those choices at the Runtime boundary. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: budgets, max turns/depth/tool concurrency, permissions, session locking, runtime cancellation, lifecycle hooks and optional steering source. - Closure path: configured limits and user corrections can change or stop one Runtime trajectory, but no standard whole-system current view → S3 judgment → organization-wide current-operation change path was found. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: hard enforcement does not imply ownership. The WithSteering contract explicitly leaves queue ownership, pending/delivered state and reconciliation to the host and inserts corrections into one run at execution boundaries; it is not a fleet/whole-system management organ. - Evidence: runtime/STEERING.md; runtime/runtime.go; runtime/partition.go. - Basis: explicit + structural. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: a host can build a manager above the library, but that external composition is outside the reviewed standard-distribution boundary.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: — - Function: no materially independent complementary-audit path over an operational claim was established. - Disturbance / variety regulated: ordinary model/tool results may be wrong or incomplete, but the standard runtime does not independently inspect operational reality and return an audit judgment to current control. - Decisive decision or feedback right: not established for S3. - Decision owner: not established. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: traces, session history, hooks, errors, tool results, compaction state and the separate runtime.Review reviewer Runtime. - Closure path: ordinary tool observations return to S1 and Review can write future memory/skills, but neither forms a claim → materially complementary evidence → independent audit verdict → corrective current-control loop. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: runtime.Review is a distinct model pass, but its supplied prompts ask what should be remembered for future sessions rather than checking a producer&#39;s operational claim against independent evidence. It is credited under S4 instead of being relabeled as audit. - Evidence: runtime/review.go; runtime/reviewprompts.go; runtime/runtime.go. - Basis: explicit + structural. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: a caller can provide a domain-specific ReviewSpec.Prompt and tools, but generic custom prompting is not a first-party S3-specific constructor.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-c\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: C - Function: convert evidence from completed user/environment interactions into durable future-facing memory or reusable skills that can alter later agent capability. - Disturbance / variety regulated: user preferences, project constraints and useful workflows discovered during operation can change or accumulate, making a fixed future agent context/capability progressively less fitted to later sessions. - Decisive decision or feedback right: judge which completed-interaction distinctions are durable/reusable enough to save and choose whether to create/update future-facing memory or procedural skill content. - Decision owner: constructor path. When invoked, the dedicated reviewer model owns the content-selection/adaptation judgment; however the host application must explicitly install the OnStop trigger, shared stores and review toolset, so Harness does not supply a resident autonomous S4 organ by default. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: runtime.Review, snapshot reviewer Runtime, ReviewPromptDefault / ReviewPromptVerbose, memory and skill tools/stores, origin tagging, RuntimeDeps.Skills, BuildRuntime skill-index construction and refreshSkills before later model requests. - Closure path: completed user/agent interaction → separately invoked reviewer reads the finished conversation → reviewer identifies a durable preference/project fact/reusable workflow → shared memory/skill write → later Runtime request refreshes the self-authored skill index / exposes load tools → subsequent operational model context and available procedural knowledge can change. - Boundary reachability: runtime.Review and the standard future-session prompts are shipped Runtime APIs with documented LifecycleHooks.OnStop wiring; the same repository ships memory/skill providers and an end-to-end example. The function-specific constructor is therefore product-reachable even though applications must opt in and compose it. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: once composed, the reviewer model makes the substantive prospective selection rather than a fixed parser. The missing piece is standard autonomous installation/trigger ownership, so Methodology 0.3.6 publishes constructor C, not A. - Evidence: runtime/review.go; runtime/reviewprompts.go; runtime/builder.go; runtime/skillsrefresh.go; examples/self-improving-agent/main.go. - Basis: explicit + structural. - Confidence: high. - External distinction: the standard reviewer prompt explicitly extracts changing user preferences and project facts from a completed interaction, treating user/project evidence outside the Runtime&#39;s fixed prior capability as distinctions worth carrying forward. - Future / prospective distinction: the prompt filters for information “worth remembering for future sessions” and reusable workflows/techniques rather than merely summarizing the completed task. - Adaptation option generated: reviewer-selected durable memory entries and named procedural skills representing future behavior/context/capability changes. - Path back into current capability / S3: reviewer writes into the parent&#39;s shared stores; BuildRuntime exposes memory/skills and embeds skill indices, while Runtime.Run refreshes self-authored skills before subsequent model requests, so later S1 operation can use the adaptation. - Caveats: memory alone would not establish S4. Credit rests on the explicit future-relevance judgment plus reusable-skill generation and a first-party return path into later model context. The opt-in caller wiring prevents A.\">C</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: — - Function: no first-party identity/ultimate-policy closure was established at the declared application recursion. - Disturbance / variety regulated: agent specs, system prompts, permissions, budgets and memory files constrain operation, but the reviewed distribution does not define a genuine identity/constitutional issue and an authoritative decision loop that returns to govern the organization. - Decisive decision or feedback right: not established for S5. - Decision owner: caller/operator configuration supplies prompts, permissions and policy-like constraints; no qualifying S5 authority path is packaged by Harness. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: AgentSpec.SystemPrompt, MemoryFiles, permission checker, budgets, steering and caller-selected runtime configuration. - Closure path: configuration can alter later operation, but no identity/ultimate-policy issue → legitimate authority judgment → returned governing-policy closure is established. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: prompts and permission gates are constraints, not S5 by themselves. runtime.Review writes memory/skills and does not receive an ultimate-policy mandate or authority to redefine the application&#39;s identity. - Evidence: runtime/builder.go; runtime/review.go; runtime/STEERING.md. - Basis: explicit + structural. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: a host application may establish its own parent governance around Harness; that would require assessment at the host application&#39;s recursion rather than attribution to this library.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/11c63c1307b12e1fb1dbc6c081f7fed561bf9b1c/assessments/harness.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit 11c63c1307b12e1fb1dbc6c081f7fed561bf9b1c\">11c63c1 &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr data-a=\"1\" data-c=\"0\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-10-30T22:43:00+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/TokenRhythm/opensquilla\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">OpenSquilla</a><small>2026</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"1\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>1</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>0</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-10-30\">2026-10-30</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/TokenRhythm/opensquilla/tree/71888b1fab67010770684ced0beffc4e91677cde\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision 71888b1fab67010770684ced0beffc4e91677cde\">71888b1 &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: transform varied user tasks into observable tool-mediated actions and conversational results. - Disturbance / variety regulated: different task demands, changing tool outputs, model errors, filesystem/web state and context pressure. - Decisive decision or feedback right: model chooses next response and tool invocation after seeing returned tool observations; the engine continues the turn loop. - Decision owner: first-party configured model-backed Agent in a standard supported execution mode; external provider supplies computation but does not own the harness&#39;s task boundary. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: TurnRunner, tool dispatcher, history/compaction, provider selection, tool policy, model and subagent budgets. - Closure path: task and context → model action/tool decision → authorized dispatch → observation recorded → next model decision or final response. - Boundary reachability: shipped Agent.runturn/shared runtime executes the loop and registers built-in tools; no dev-only evaluator is needed for the ordinary task cycle. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: agent model chooses semantic task/action content; deterministic runtime admits, executes and bounds its choices. - Evidence: engine/agent.py, engine/runtime.py, tools/dispatch.py. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: high for shipped architecture, not task-quality measurements. - Caveats: exact tool reachability depends on configured permission and provider availability.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: — - Function: no established dedicated regulation of interference among distinct S1 units at this assessed recursion. - Disturbance / variety regulated: concurrent child execution and shared-resource hazards exist, but no concrete inter-S1 oscillation or conflicting operational commitments are shown to be corrected by a specific first-party coordination feedback relation. - Decisive decision or feedback right: SubagentManager imposes maximum concurrent children and depth and records completion; subagents can list/steer/kill children. These are scheduling and intervention tools rather than an evidenced S2-specific reconciliation judgment. - Decision owner: external operator sets limits; model can choose child tasks, subject to runtime enforcement. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: SubagentRegistry, asyncio tasks, status records, timeout/cancel, per-session origin checks and permission gating. - Closure path: child results return to its caller, but an independently identifiable interference → coordination → changed subsequent S1 operation path was not located. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: parallel agency and a supervising tool are not themselves the Profile S2 function. - Evidence: engine/subagent.py, tools/builtin/agents.py, telemetry/coordination.py. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: medium for bounded shipped modes. - Caveats: applications built on the tools may add S2, but that cannot be borrowed by the standard harness.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-unknown\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: ? - Function: potential present control over a parent agent&#39;s active children and persistent goals, but the exact whole-system view and accountability boundary remain unresolved. - Disturbance / variety regulated: child progress/failure, concurrent task pressure, goal status and session limits. - Decisive decision or feedback right: optional agent-accessible subagents.list, .kill and .steer can redirect active child work; goalcontrol can update progress, but status transitions are constrained by user authority. - Decision owner: potentially the parent agent when permissions expose tools; user owns objective and several status rights, runtime enforces the policies. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: session list, task runtime send/cancel, GoalService, status records and stored configured budgets. - Closure path: steering commands can reach child sessions and affect their next input; it is not yet established that a whole-of-system current view drives a shared resource/commitment decision in a supported standard mode. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: agent can choose an intervention once allowed, but tool affordances alone do not prove a metasystem-wide S3 function; ordinary goal tracking and termination are insufficient. - Evidence: tools/builtin/agents.py, tools/builtin/goalcontrol.py, engine/subagent.py. - Basis: structural for tools, unknown for whole-system S3 control. - Confidence: low for S3 ownership. - Caveats: resolve whether the permitted operating mode provides a real whole-of-parent work view and integrated current-control decisions, not merely optional agent intervention endpoints.\">?</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: — - Function: no independent complementary direct-access audit of operating claims identified in the shipped agent run. - Disturbance / variety regulated: tool failures and unsafe operations are handled by ordinary policy checks and logs; they do not automatically constitute complementary audit. - Decisive decision or feedback right: middleware, tool guards, runtime events, telemetry and tests validate/enforce or observe the same operating stream rather than an independently owned corrective inspection channel. - Decision owner: runtime guardrails, developer/operator config and CI testers; no independent audited judgment/return owner at the assessed boundary. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: sandbox approval, tool policy, runtime event logging, diagnostics, fault-injection tests. - Closure path: guard denials feed the ordinary task loop; no separate audit-to-S3 corrective feedback is evidenced. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: a model checking its own tool result is ordinary operation, not necessarily independent S3 inspection. - Evidence: sandbox/approvalruntime.py, tools/policyruntime.py, engine/runtimeevents.py. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: medium. - Caveats: an operator might provide external auditing; it is not part of the credited first-party assessment boundary.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: — - Function: no evidenced prospectively oriented environment-sensing, alternative-strategy choice and feedback into current organizational capability. - Disturbance / variety regulated: variation in per-request complexity, provider health and context pressure drives present-turn routing, not a prospective outside-and-then strategy cycle. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Squilla router chooses tier/response-mode under operator rollout and capability policy; optional model hold changes later turns within a session. - Decision owner: configured router model/policy for task-level routing and user/operator for available tiers; no identified separate S4 adaptation authority. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: RoutingPolicyEngine, calibration store, model capability catalog, RouterControlHoldStore, snapshots and budget gates. - Closure path: routing decisions affect next request/turn, but there is no evidenced future environmental distinction → strategic option → capability change through S3 return. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: model-driven tier selection and dynamic tool/skill use are operational optimization; neither alone satisfies prospective S4. - Evidence: engine/steps/squillarouter.py, routercontrol.py, tools/builtin/routercontrol.py. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: medium. - Caveats: source has substantial model-routing feedback; a strategic adaptation extension could alter this judgment if supplied in the first-party supported mode.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: — - Function: no installed internal or parent-governed ultimate-policy/identity decision-and-return cycle at the declared agent/session recursion. - Disturbance / variety regulated: tool permissions, budget, routing overrides and user tasks are bounded by settings; they are not a system identity conflict. - Decisive decision or feedback right: users/operators set runtime constraints and grant approvals; goalcontrol expressly reserves objective/status changes to the user in critical cases. - Decision owner: human user/operator for configuration and objectives, without a first-party legitimate identity-level issue/ratification loop. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: config/policy files, permission validators, sandbox and goal service state. - Closure path: ordinary tool approval/denial changes execution; no policy-level issue reaches ultimate authority and returns as a governing identity decision. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: tool safety and prompt-level instructions enforce existing policy; neither grants the model an ultimate-policy constitutional right nor builds a parent S5 closure. - Evidence: tools/builtin/goalcontrol.py, sandbox/policystore.py, tools/policyruntime.py. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: medium. - Caveats: project maintainer governance and end-user choice remain outside this app&#39;s run-level identity-governing circuit.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/6c7ec5c66edf0ff28010169a3013de5d147e66a4/assessments/op/opensquilla.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit 6c7ec5c66edf0ff28010169a3013de5d147e66a4\">6c7ec5c &#8599;</a></td></tr>"];
